WEBVTT

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:23.730
<v Ira Glass>Justina Tedesco knows exactly when she got the idea to leave suburban New Jersey and move to a different state. It was still at the height of COVID. Her second-grader was doing remote learning. The vaccine had barely been introduced, and most people couldn't get it.
<v Ira Glass>Justina Tedesco 清楚地记得她是何时萌生了离开新泽西州郊区搬到另一个州的念头。那时正处于新冠疫情最严重的时期，她的二年级孩子还在上网课。疫苗才刚刚推出，大多数人还打不上。

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:27.380
<v Ira Glass>She was working from home, and her son's class took a snack break.
<v Ira Glass>她当时在家工作，她儿子的班级刚好休息吃零食。

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:52.110
<v Justina Tedesco>So we would usually go into the kitchen, grab a snack. And on the TV was, of course, Fox News. And DeSantis was on the TV talking about the pandemic and how he disapproved of how other states were handling things. And he said, he goes, well, since we're handling things so differently, if you're not happy in the state you live in, come to Florida. We welcome you here.
<v Justina Tedesco>我们通常会去厨房拿点零食，电视上放的当然是福克斯新闻（Fox News）。德桑蒂斯（DeSantis）在电视上谈论疫情，说他不同意其他州处理问题的方式。他说，既然我们要处理的方式截然不同，如果你对自己居住的州不满意，那就来佛罗里达州吧，我们欢迎你。

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:58.130
<v Ron DeSantis>If you look at home sales, I mean, it's pretty clear that people are viewing Florida as a landing pad.
<v Ron DeSantis>如果你看看房屋销售情况，很明显，人们正把佛罗里达州视为一个落脚点。

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:04.370
<v Ira Glass>When we looked up the clip, DeSantis didn't exactly tell people to move to Florida. But you can see how Justina took it that way.
<v Ira Glass>当我们查看那个片段时，德桑蒂斯并没有确切地告诉人们搬到佛罗里达州。但你可以看出 Justina 是如何理解的。

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:26.670
<v Ron DeSantis>You know, Tucker, it's interesting. As this went on, I would encounter people, particularly in September, that would move from parts of the country, say, look, they closed our schools. I want-- your schools are open. I'm going to come here because you guys are doing it right. And I really do think these lockdowns have driven some people to Florida who just had enough with these draconian, ineffective restrictions.
<v Ron DeSantis>你知道，塔克（Tucker），这很有趣。随着时间的推移，我遇到了一些人，特别是在九月，他们从全国各地搬来，说：“看，他们关闭了我们的学校。我想……你们的学校是开放的。我要搬到这里，因为你们做得对。”我确实认为这些封锁措施迫使一些人搬到了佛罗里达州，他们对这些严苛且无效的限制已经受够了。

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:35.400
<v Justina Tedesco>I don't know, it was like music to our ears. And I thought about it for a second. And I'm like, could you imagine us living in Florida? Could we do it? Could we just pick up everything and move?
<v Justina Tedesco>我不知道，那听起来就像悦耳的音乐。我考虑了一会儿。我在想，你能想象我们住在佛罗里达州吗？我们能做到吗？我们能收拾好一切搬过去吗？

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:50.020
<v Ira Glass>The big thing she wanted, in-person learning for her son. For Dennis and Alenda Michael, in a small town in Washington State, it was a story on NBC News around the same time that did the job. The story was about a grocery store in Naples, Florida.
<v Ira Glass>她最想要的是让儿子参加线下学习。对于居住在华盛顿州一个小镇的 Dennis 和 Alenda Michael 夫妇来说，大约在同一时间，NBC 新闻的一篇报道起到了作用。那篇报道讲的是佛罗里达州那不勒斯市的一家杂货店。

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.270
<v Dennis Michael>And it was a hit piece on them because everybody was walking around the store without their mask on.
<v Dennis Michael>那是一篇针对他们的批评报道，因为店里的每个人都没戴口罩就在店里走来走去。

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.130
<v Ira Glass>In fact, the reporter in the story reads comments from people who saw video of this store online.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，报道中的记者读了一些网友在网上看到这家店的视频后的评论。

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:13.020
<v Reporter>Prompting mostly heavy criticism. "I can't stop watching this. My jaw is literally on my desk. This should be a crime. People across the country are dying because of behavior like this." And from a woman mourning her father's death from COVID--
<v Reporter>这引发了大部分严厉的批评。“我无法停止观看这个视频，我的下巴简直惊掉在桌子上了。这应该是一种犯罪。全国各地的人们因为这种行为而死去。”还有一位哀悼父亲死于新冠的女士——

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.630
<v Dennis Michael>And I thought, that's a place I want to be.
<v Dennis Michael>我想，那才是我想要去的地方。

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:24.540
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, wait. I just want to be sure I understand. There was a story on television saying, look how terrible these people are. They're walking around without masks. And you saw that, and you concluded--
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等，等等。我只是想确保我理解对了。电视上有一篇报道说，看这些人多糟糕啊。他们没戴口罩走来走去。你看到了，然后你就得出结论——

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:40.690
<v Dennis Michael>That's where I want to be. The "terrible people part" I ignored because it looked to me like there were people that were enjoying life. And it just was a sense of freedom that we didn't have in Washington State.
<v Dennis Michael>那是我想去的地方。我忽略了“糟糕的人”那部分，因为在我看来，那里的人们在享受生活。这正是我们在华盛顿州所没有的一种自由感。

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:57.370
<v Ira Glass>I was curious about all this because in the speeches that Ron DeSantis is giving as he runs for president, he talks a lot about how Florida is the fastest-growing state, which it is. And he talks about how people are moving to Florida because of him and all he's done there, which, who knows?
<v Ira Glass>我对这一切感到好奇，因为罗恩·德桑蒂斯（Ron DeSantis）在竞选总统时发表的演讲中，经常谈到佛罗里达州是增长最快的州，事实确实如此。他还谈到人们因为他和他所做的一切而搬到佛罗里达州，谁知道呢？

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:13.880
<v Ira Glass>It's true that among the transplants, there are twice as many Republicans as Democrats. But I wondered, what is really happening? Was DeSantis actually the reason that people uprooted their lives and crossed the country, like they wanted to be part of his Florida experiment?
<v Ira Glass>事实确实是，在这些移居者中，共和党人是民主党人的两倍。但我很好奇，到底发生了什么？德桑蒂斯真的是人们背井离乡、跨越全国的原因吗，就像他们想参与他的佛罗里达实验一样？

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:28.840
<v Ira Glass>So in the last week, I've talked to a half dozen people who told me, yes, they did exactly that. Though they all said it wasn't just politics. It was also the fact that there's no state income tax in Florida, and housing prices, for nearly all of them, that's way cheaper than where they moved from.
<v Ira Glass>所以在过去一周里，我采访了六个人，他们告诉我，是的，他们确实是这样做的。尽管他们都说这不仅仅是政治原因，还因为佛罗里达州没有州所得税，而且对于他们几乎所有人来说，这里的房价远低于他们搬离的地方。

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:51.380
<v Ira Glass>They love the sunny, summery lifestyle. But they all said politics clinched it, in particular, COVID politics, the way Governor Ron DeSantis broke ranks early and loudly from other states in opening schools and ditching mask mandates. And that's when migration to the state picked up. As one woman, Kim, who moved from Payson, Utah, put it--
<v Ira Glass>他们热爱阳光明媚、充满夏日气息的生活方式。但他们都说政治因素起了决定性作用，尤其是新冠疫情政治，罗恩·德桑蒂斯州长在开放学校和废除口罩强制令方面，很早就大声地与其它州划清了界限。从那时起，迁入该州的人数开始增加。正如从犹他州佩森搬来的 Kim 所说——

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.810
<v Kim>We honestly had never even considered Florida prior to COVID.
<v Kim>老实说，在新冠疫情之前，我们甚至从未考虑过佛罗里达州。

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:56.800
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:03.780
<v Kim>Yeah, it had not been on our list at all.
<v Kim>是的，它根本不在我们的名单上。

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:17.330
<v Ira Glass>Justina wanted to get her second-grader on board with the idea of moving. So before they put their house on the market, she sat down with him on the living room couch, and they each put a piece of paper on the coffee table, and each wrote out the pros and cons of going to Florida.
<v Ira Glass>Justina 想让她上二年级的孩子接受搬家的想法。所以，在他们把房子挂牌出售之前，她和他坐在客厅的沙发上，每人在茶几上放了一张纸，写下了搬到佛罗里达州的利弊。

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:30.370
<v Justina Tedesco>And I said, OK, well, Vincent, let's sit down and let's write down all the things that come to your mind about what makes Florida awesome, and then what we would might miss if we move away from New Jersey. And so he goes, OK.
<v Justina Tedesco>我说：“好吧，文森特（Vincent），我们坐下来写下你脑海中关于佛罗里达州为什么棒的所有想法，然后写下如果我们离开新泽西州会错过什么。”他说，好的。

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:54.520
<v Justina Tedesco>So my list started with, obviously, at the top of mine was in-person learning. And then I had on my list, no state income tax. I wrote DeSantis down as one of the items. He was literally just-- his name was listed as number 3. And some of my cons were missing our family, not having the convenience of being able to have babysitters as frequently--
<v Justina Tedesco>所以我的清单开头，我的首要因素显然是线下学习。然后我的清单上还有没有州所得税。我把德桑蒂斯作为其中一项写了下来。他简直就是——他的名字被列为第三项。而我的一些弊端是想念家人，没有像以前那样方便地请到保姆——

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.000
<v Ira Glass>She has a big Italian family living around them in New Jersey.
<v Ira Glass>她的大家族住在新泽西州的周围。

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:10.900
<v Justina Tedesco>And when I went to go review my son's list, and I said, hey, what do you have, his list had-- his pros were lizards, were on his pros list. And I think I actually had that on my cons list.
<v Justina Tedesco>当我去检查我儿子的清单时，我说：“嘿，你写了什么？”他的优点清单上有——蜥蜴。我想我实际上把那个写在了我的弊端清单上。

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:11.530
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.680
<v Justina Tedesco>And on his cons list, he wrote sharks.
<v Justina Tedesco>而在他的弊端清单上，他写了鲨鱼。

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:30.520
<v Ira Glass>But the most important thing, he didn't write that he would miss his classmates or his school, which told Justina his remote learning experience was so bad, he wasn't bonding with those kids, and they really got to get out of New Jersey and into in-person school.
<v Ira Glass>但最重要的是，他没有写他会想念同学或学校，这告诉 Justina，他的远程学习体验太糟糕了，他与那些孩子没有建立起联系，他们真的必须离开新泽西州，去上实体学校。

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:55.990
<v Ira Glass>While COVID policies were the thing that really convinced the people I talked to into moving, almost all of them liked the other stuff that Ron DeSantis has so prominently brought to Florida, his anti-woke policies, restricting what's said in schools about sexuality and race and systemic racism, his ban on gender-affirming care for trans kids, and on transgender girls doing sports with other girls, his ban on abortion after six weeks. Justina weighs up the trade-offs this way.
<v Ira Glass>虽然新冠政策是真正说服我采访的人搬家的主要因素，但他们几乎所有人都喜欢罗恩·德桑蒂斯在佛罗里达州大力推行的其他政策，比如他的反“觉醒”（anti-woke）政策，限制在学校讨论性倾向、种族和系统性种族主义；禁止为跨性别儿童提供性别肯定护理，禁止跨性别女孩参加女子体育比赛；禁止在怀孕六周后进行堕胎。Justina 是这样衡量取舍的。

00:05:55.990 --> 00:06:13.270
<v Justina Tedesco>I do miss all my family. And I said to myself too, you know, I'd rather be where I have most things in common with people. And I can always visit New Jersey or New York on vacation. [CHUCKLES]
<v Justina Tedesco>我确实很想念我所有的家人。我也对自己说，你知道，我宁愿和志趣相投的人生活在一起。而且我度假时总能回新泽西州或纽约看看。[轻笑]

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:27.260
<v Ira Glass>For today on our program, Ron DeSantis is running around the country telling Americans we can all have what these new Floridians are loving in their new homes if we vote for him for president. Maybe you've seen the bumper sticker, "Make America Florida."
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目中，罗恩·德桑蒂斯正在全国各地告诉美国人，如果我们投票选他当总统，我们都能拥有这些新佛罗里达人在新家所喜爱的一切。也许你见过那个保险杠贴纸，“让美国变成佛罗里达”（Make America Florida）。

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:46.040
<v Ira Glass>So what would that be like? How's it working out for people? Today, we go inside the Florida experiment he's created there. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>那么那会是什么样子呢？人们的生活过得怎么样？今天，我们深入了解他在那里创建的佛罗里达实验。来自芝加哥 WBEZ 电台，这是《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:06:46.040 --> 00:07:05.780
<v Ira Glass>Act one, Prescription for Freedom. So Ron DeSantis' governorship, and also the presence of Donald Trump in the state, have created a kind of hothouse of a political experiment on the right in Florida, with all kinds of wealthy influential right-wingers migrating to the state, specifically to Sarasota County, northwest of Mar-a-Lago over on the Gulf Coast.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，自由处方。罗恩·德桑蒂斯的州长任期，以及唐纳德·特朗普在该州的存在，在佛罗里达州创造了一个右翼政治实验的温床，各种富有的、有影响力的右翼分子迁往该州，特别是迁往海湖庄园西北部的萨拉索塔县（Sarasota County），那边在墨西哥湾沿岸。

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:24.080
<v Ira Glass>Perfect example, Mike Flynn is one of the newest arrivals. He moved to Englewood in Sarasota County. You might remember Mike Flynn. President Trump appointed him as national security advisor, and he lasted only 23 days in that job. He got kicked out after getting in trouble for lying to the FBI about talking to the Russian ambassador.
<v Ira Glass>最好的例子就是，迈克·弗林（Mike Flynn）是最新抵达的人之一。他搬到了萨拉索塔县的恩格尔伍德（Englewood）。你可能还记得迈克·弗林。特朗普总统任命他为国家安全顾问，但他只干了 23 天。在因与俄罗斯大使通话的问题对联邦调查局撒谎而惹上麻烦后，他被解职了。

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:47.010
<v Ira Glass>In Florida, he started a whole new life, waging a kind of information war and a political war on the far right. And Sarasota is the right place for a guy like that to live out his dreams. Steve Bannon, the co-founder of Moms for Liberty, the headquarters of Rumble, of Truth Social-- I could go on-- Charlie Kirk, Patrick Byrne, Doug Logan, they all have ties to Sarasota County. Tucker Carlson is just down the coast.
<v Ira Glass>在佛罗里达州，他开始了全新的生活，在极右翼阵营发动了一场信息战和政治战。萨拉索塔对于这样一个家伙来说，确实是实现梦想的好地方。“自由妈妈”（Moms for Liberty）联合创始人史蒂夫·班农（Steve Bannon）、Rumble 的总部、Truth Social 的总部——我不胜枚举——查理·柯克（Charlie Kirk）、帕特里克·伯恩（Patrick Byrne）、道格·洛根（Doug Logan），他们都与萨拉索塔县有联系。塔克·卡尔森（Tucker Carlson）就在沿岸不远处。

00:07:47.010 --> 00:08:16.970
<v Ira Glass>For whatever reason, Sarasota County, which in the past was a wealthy, white area for old-fashioned pro-business Republican types, has now become a place where these MAGA right wing rich guys have set about building their ideal version of society. And at Flynn's side, there's this one guy in particular who's been bounding from one project to the next, creating this little parallel universe down there that we're going to talk about in this next story because this guy's a pioneer in this movement that you're going to be hearing a lot about in this next election. Maybe you've already heard of it, medical freedom.
<v Ira Glass>无论出于什么原因，萨拉索塔县过去是一个老派、亲商业的共和党类型的富裕白人聚居区，现在已经变成了一个让这些 MAGA（让美国再次伟大）右翼富豪们着手建立他们理想社会版本的地方。在弗林身边，有一个特别的人，他不断地从一个项目跳到另一个项目，在那里创造了这个平行宇宙，我们将在下一段故事中谈到，因为这家伙是这场运动的先驱，你们在接下来的选举中会经常听到它。也许你们已经听说过它了：医疗自由。

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:30.650
<v Ira Glass>In Ron DeSantis' case for being president, "Make America Florida," medical freedom is a big item. One of our producers, Zoe Chace, was very interested to understand its appeal and growing popularity, and spent some time down in Sarasota County.
<v Ira Glass>在罗恩·德桑蒂斯的总统竞选诉求“让美国变成佛罗里达”中，医疗自由是一个重大议题。我们的制作人之一佐伊·蔡斯（Zoe Chace）非常想了解它的吸引力和日益增长的欢迎度，并在萨拉索塔县待了一段时间。

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:44.180
<v Zoe Chace>The guy I'm here to talk about is named Vic Mellor. He has a bunch of money, likes to spend it on politics. Just a few months ago, he paid $3 million for a building in Sarasota County, 959 East Venice Avenue.
<v Zoe Chace>我要谈的那个家伙叫维克·梅勒（Vic Mellor）。他很有钱，喜欢把钱花在政治上。就在几个月前，他在萨拉索塔县花了 300 万美元买下了一栋楼，地址是东威尼斯大道 959 号。

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:44.960
<v Vic Mellor>And then here's the studio--
<v Vic Mellor>然后这是录音室——

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:54.320
<v Zoe Chace>He's teaming up with Mike Flynn and some others to use it for this hybrid office down here. One side of it is a production house for the launch of Flynn's upcoming podcast.
<v Zoe Chace>他正在和迈克·弗林以及其他人合作，在这里使用这个混合办公空间。其中一侧是弗林即将推出的播客的制作室。

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:58.070
<v Vic Mellor>And then here's the studio where General Flynn is going to start his--
<v Vic Mellor>然后这是弗林将军即将开始他的——

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:05.610
<v Zoe Chace>This is really nice. Like, most people with their little shows, it's not like this. This is a big studio space.
<v Zoe Chace>这真的很不错。大多数做这种小节目的人，环境都不像这样。这是一个很大的录音棚空间。

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:11.900
<v Vic Mellor>Well, I guess it's good that I have no experience in it, then, huh? So I come out swinging hard.
<v Vic Mellor>好吧，那我猜我没经验是件好事，对吧？所以我一上来就会拼尽全力。

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:12.980
<v Vic Mellor>[LAUGHTER]
<v Vic Mellor>[笑声]

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:13.580
<v Zoe Chace>Wow.
<v Zoe Chace>哇。

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:21.490
<v Vic Mellor>Well, what would you expect for General Flynn? He's going to have top of the line-- I'm going to give him top-of-the-line everything, you know?
<v Vic Mellor>好吧，你对弗林将军有什么期待呢？他会有顶级的——我会给他提供所有顶级的东西，你知道吗？

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:36.210
<v Zoe Chace>Vic is a big guy. He ducks through doors. He's tall. He's wide. He dresses like he just walked off a construction site, which he did. He runs a precast concrete business. He makes the huge blocks of hollowed-out concrete that make up buildings.
<v Zoe Chace>维克是个大个子。他弯腰进门，又高又壮。他的穿着就像刚从工地走出来一样，事实也确实如此。他经营着一家预制混凝土公司。他制造那些构成建筑物的大型空心混凝土块。

00:09:36.210 --> 00:10:09.510
<v Zoe Chace>He dreams big, which brings me to the other half of 959 East Venice, the part I really want to tell you about. The place where Vic and his movement are inventing something new. Specifically, the other half of this building is going to be a medical clinic, We The People Health and Wellness Center. "We the People," drawn in Founding Fathers cursive on the big glass doors. A clinic that caters to people who did not get the COVID vaccine, who do not trust the medical establishment, and want a place to go where they can be treated by people who think like they do.
<v Zoe Chace>他梦想远大，这让我想到东威尼斯大道 959 号的另一半，那是我真正想告诉你们的部分。维克和他的运动正在那里发明某种新东西。具体来说，这栋楼的另一半将是一个医疗诊所，“我们人民健康与保健中心”（We The People Health and Wellness Center）。在巨大的玻璃门上用国父们的草书体写着“我们人民”。这是一个专门为那些没有接种新冠疫苗、不信任医疗机构、想要在一个能够被志同道合的人治疗的地方就医的人提供的诊所。

