<v Zoe Chace>在这些节目中阐述的基本阴谋论是这样的——新冠病毒是由比尔·盖茨和世界卫生组织一手策划的人造病毒，目的是控制世界人口。推动疫苗是为了杀死我们。封锁是为了控制我们。Moderna、辉瑞，他们都参与其中。他们想消灭伊维菌素，因为它是他们正在销售的药物的廉价替代品。

00:19:53.330 --> 00:20:01.960
<v Zoe Chace>The conspiracy has a name. It's called The Great Reset. And that's what Flynn will be covering on his show down the hall from the clinic, among other things.
<v Zoe Chace>这个阴谋有个名字。它被称为“大重置”（The Great Reset）。这就是弗林会在诊所大厅的另一头在他的节目中讨论的内容，除了一些其他事情。

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:10.470
<v Clay Clark>General Flynn, back to you, sir. What is this vaccination campaign truly all about, for anybody who may be hearing this for the first time?
<v Clay Clark>弗林将军，回到您这里，先生。对于任何可能是第一次听到这个消息的人来说，这次疫苗接种运动到底是为了什么？

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:24.560
<v Michael Flynn>What we are facing is, we are facing a globalist takeover of every freedom-loving country in the world, and they're using this vaccination to control us.
<v Michael Flynn>我们面临的是，我们正面临每一个热爱自由的国家被全球主义者接管，他们正在利用这种疫苗来控制我们。

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:39.700
<v Zoe Chace>When I ask Vic and Tanya straight-up if they believe in the Great Reset, they were both like, I'm not going to cop to that exact thing, like all the stuff you said. Specifically, like, maybe it wasn't Bill Gates. But, yes, there was a power grab, and there was money to be made.
<v Zoe Chace>当我直接问维克和坦尼娅他们是否相信“大重置”时，他们都说，我不会承认确切的事情，就像你说的那些。具体来说，也许不是比尔·盖茨。但是，是的，有人在夺权，而且有利可图。

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:53.750
<v Tanya Parus>And if you think about that, that doesn't mean that you're a conspiracy theorist. That just means that there's greedy people in the world, which we all know. It doesn't mean that I'm a bad person because I believe our government is more corrupt than you do.
<v Tanya Parus>如果你仔细想想，这并不意味着你是一个阴谋论者。这只意味着世界上有贪婪的人，我们都知道这一点。我不因为相信我们的政府比你认为的更腐败，就变成一个坏人。

00:20:53.750 --> 00:21:10.930
<v Vic Mellor>This is a patriotic movement. It is. I'm going to say, fuck this globalist shit, you know? Seriously. I'm totally against it.
<v Vic Mellor>这是一场爱国运动。确实是。我要说，去你的全球主义烂事，你知道吗？严肃点。我完全反对它。

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:29.770
<v Zoe Chace>It makes sense to me that Tanya was the person who threw herself into this project of building this alternative medical system, and now running the We The People Clinic at 959 East Venice. She's definitely a take-matters-into-your-own-hands type. And she feels like doctors haven't listened to her in the past when she needed them.
<v Zoe Chace>在我看来，坦尼娅是那种投身于建立这个替代医疗系统项目的人，现在又在管理东威尼斯大道 959 号的“我们人民诊所”，这很合理。她绝对是那种“把事情掌握在自己手中”的人。而且她觉得过去当她需要医生时，他们并没有听她的。

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:38.480
<v Tanya Parus>I basically have a 21-year-old son, and I have a 9 and a 7. So I had 13 miscarriages in between.
<v Tanya Parus>我基本上有一个 21 岁的儿子，还有 9 岁和 7 岁的孩子。所以在这期间我流产了 13 次。

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:39.170
<v Zoe Chace>Oh, my god.
<v Zoe Chace>天哪。

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:45.670
<v Tanya Parus>Yeah. That was-- yeah, that was awful. That was an awful time in my life. But--
<v Tanya Parus>是的。那是——是的，那太可怕了。那是我生命中一段可怕的时光。但是——

00:21:45.670 --> 00:21:48.510
<v Zoe Chace>I'm so sorry. That's very, very hard.
<v Zoe Chace>我非常抱歉。这非常、非常困难。

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:56.810
<v Tanya Parus>It was awful. So, obviously, and I'm research, research, research, research. That's all I do. What I do is I dig in.
<v Tanya Parus>那太可怕了。所以，显然，我一直在研究、研究、研究、研究。这就是我所做的一切。我所做的就是钻研。

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:59.990
<v Zoe Chace>You wanted to solve that, figure that out.
<v Zoe Chace>你想解决那个问题，搞清楚它。

00:21:59.990 --> 00:22:04.490
<v Tanya Parus>I like to solve everything, try to at least have an understanding for myself.
<v Tanya Parus>我喜欢解决所有事情，至少尝试为自己找到一个理解。

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:18.410
<v Zoe Chace>Nine miscarriages in, Tanya seemed to find a doctor who figured out something that might help. And then she had two kids right in a row, the kids who years later would go on to flick masks onto the bathroom floor during COVID.
<v Zoe Chace>流产了九次之后，坦尼娅似乎找到了一位医生，想出了一些可能对她有帮助的方法。然后她接连生了两个孩子，也就是几年后在新冠期间把口罩扔在浴室地板上的那两个孩子。

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:28.110
<v Tanya Parus>I didn't like that it took me nine miscarriages before anyone cared to figure out what the heck was wrong with me.
<v Tanya Parus>我不喜欢流产了九次才有人愿意花心思去弄清楚我到底出了什么问题。

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:51.030
<v Zoe Chace>What we're chronicling here, remember, is the establishment of a headquarters of this new MAGA-fied medical freedom movement at 959 East Venice Avenue, half podcast studio, half medical clinic. And the next big moment in the story of the creation of this building happens at arguably the opposite of this place, the main hospital in the area, Sarasota Memorial Hospital.
<v Zoe Chace>记住，我们在这里记录的是这场新的 MAGA（让美国再次伟大）化“医疗自由”运动总部的建立，就在东威尼斯大道 959 号，一半是播客录音室，一半是医疗诊所。在这个大楼创建的故事中，下一个重大时刻发生在这个地方的对立面——当地的主要医院，萨拉索塔纪念医院（Sarasota Memorial Hospital）。

00:22:51.030 --> 00:23:13.350
<v Zoe Chace>Not long after the mask mandate event at The Hollow, Vic started up a political organizing company to support what he calls "freedom candidates," America-first candidates, essentially, Trumpists. He called it The Hollow 2A and threw a lot of money into it. They supported a slate of school board candidates who swept into office. They also backed another group of candidates for a local hospital.
<v Zoe Chace>在“空洞”的口罩强制令事件后不久，维克创办了一家政治组织公司，以支持他所说的“自由候选人”，即美国优先候选人，基本上就是特朗普主义者。他称之为“空洞 2A”，并投入了大量资金。他们支持了一批校董会候选人，这些人横扫了选举。他们还支持了另一组当地医院的候选人。

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:38.410
<v Zoe Chace>Sarasota Memorial Hospital is governed by a publicly elected board, and they supported four candidates who were aligned with this medical freedom movement. These candidates were basically anti-everything the hospital was doing, anti the CDC protocols they used, anti the vaccines they administered, upset that the hospital didn't offer ivermectin and other alternative medicines to patients who wanted it to treat COVID.
<v Zoe Chace>萨拉索塔纪念医院由一个民选委员会管理，他们支持了四名与这场医疗自由运动立场一致的候选人。这些候选人基本上反对医院正在做的一切，反对他们使用的 CDC 规程，反对他们接种的疫苗，对医院没有为想要治疗新冠的病人提供伊维菌素和其他替代药物感到不满。

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:56.870
<v Zoe Chace>They kind of wanted to tear the whole place down, and they called for a big investigation into everything that had transpired during COVID. Three of their preferred candidates won, not enough to control the board, enough to make some noise. The public hospital has public meetings every month, and you can imagine how those have been going.
<v Zoe Chace>他们简直想把整个地方推倒，他们呼吁对新冠期间发生的一切进行大规模调查。他们支持的三名候选人赢了，虽然不足以控制委员会，但足以制造一些动静。这家公立医院每个月都有公开会议，你可以想象那些会议是怎么进行的。

00:23:56.870 --> 00:23:59.230
<v Hospital Board Member>So let's begin our meeting.
<v Hospital Board Member>那么，让我们开始我们的会议。

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:22.990
<v Zoe Chace>In February of this year, 2023, the board was delivering the results of a three-year review of the hospital's performance during the COVID pandemic. The report was glowing. The hospital's COVID-19 death rate was about 25% lower than state and national averages. Hospital stays were shorter too, which is a pretty great verdict for a place full of elderly people.
<v Zoe Chace>今年 2 月，也就是 2023 年，委员会发布了对医院在大流行期间表现的三年评估结果。报告评价很高。医院的新冠死亡率比州和全国平均水平低约 25%。住院时间也更短，对于一个满是老年人的地方来说，这真是一个伟大的裁决。

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:42.260
<v Zoe Chace>Vic and The Hollow crew didn't believe those results at all. They started pushing for a completely independent investigation of the hospital's COVID practices. Kind of like those forensic audits that people who thought the election was stolen wanted after 2020. Demands to comb through every ballot, every gear of the voting machines.
<v Zoe Chace>维克和“空洞”的那帮人根本不相信这些结果。他们开始推动对医院的新冠实践进行彻底的独立调查。有点像那些认为选举被窃的人在 2020 年之后想要的那些法医审计。要求仔细检查每一张选票，投票机的每一个齿轮。

00:24:42.260 --> 00:24:48.370
<v Zoe Chace>But this, of course, was way more personal because there was all this grief and pain mixed in with the suspicion.
<v Zoe Chace>但这当然更具个人色彩，因为怀疑中夹杂着太多的悲痛和痛苦。

00:24:48.370 --> 00:25:08.830
<v Tanya Parus>When we had people getting up there crying and saying, all we want to do is, I want answers. I want to sit down with the-- I want to understand why my husband died, why you wouldn't let me come in there, why you wouldn't use ivermectin. Why did you give her remdesivir when I told you not to give her remdesivir? All of these things were coming up.
<v Tanya Parus>当我们有人们站在那里哭泣并说，我们想要的只是，我想要答案。我想和——我想明白为什么我丈夫死了，为什么你们不让我进去，为什么你们不用伊维菌素。为什么当我告诉你们不要给她瑞德西韦（remdesivir）时，你们却给她用了瑞德西韦？所有这些事情都冒出来了。

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:14.140
<v Woman>My son was 39 years of age and ended up in the hospital.
<v Woman>我儿子 39 岁，最后进了医院。

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:20.080
<v Zoe Chace>"My son was 39 years of age when he went into the hospital," this one woman is saying up at the mic.
<v Zoe Chace>“我儿子进医院时 39 岁，”这位女士站在麦克风前说道。

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:45.690
<v Woman>We weren't allowed with him. He was alone. He was alone. And we had a prescription of ivermectin that was very hard to fill. We finally got it filled. We brought it in, and they refused it. And it's like, where is the team anymore? There's no team. It's like, doctors are making the decisions for all of us.
<v Woman>我们不允许陪他。他孤身一人。他孤身一人。我们有伊维菌素的处方，很难配到。我们终于配到了。我们带了进去，他们拒绝了。这就好像，团队在哪里？没有团队。就像是，医生在为我们所有人做决定。

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:49.220
<v Zoe Chace>"It's like the doctors are making decisions for all of us."
<v Zoe Chace>“就像医生在为我们所有人做决定。”

00:25:49.220 --> 00:26:01.230
<v Woman>They said his organs are failing. He's 39. What do you mean his organs are failing? We asked for ivermectin. I had my lawyer on the phone.
<v Woman>他们说他的器官在衰竭。他才 39 岁。你们什么意思说他的器官在衰竭？我们要求伊维菌素。我打电话让我的律师在通话中。

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:21.530
<v Zoe Chace>"They said his organs are failing. He's 39. What do you mean his organs are failing? We asked for ivermectin. I had my lawyer on the phone. They refused. All the nurses and doctors," she goes on, "I know how hard you work. But the CDC protocol, it's killing people."
<v Zoe Chace>“他们拒绝了。所有的护士和医生，”她继续说，“我知道你们工作有多努力。但 CDC 的规程正在害死人。”

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:42.710
<v Zoe Chace>Dr. Manny Gordillo ran the COVID response at the hospital. He was at that hearing, but didn't speak. Ivermectin came up in so much of the testimony that I wanted to talk to him about that. It was a stand-in for so many things, but it was also just a drug, a relatively harmless drug. Why not just give them the ivermectin?
<v Zoe Chace>曼尼·戈迪略（Manny Gordillo）医生负责该医院的新冠应对工作。他在那次听证会上，但没有发言。伊维菌素在这么多证词中被提及，我想和他谈谈这个。它是许多事物的代名词，但它也只是一种药，一种相对无害的药。为什么不干脆给他们伊维菌素呢？

00:26:42.710 --> 00:27:18.920
<v Manny Gordillo>So ivermectin, to me, is just a symbol. It's a word. When they did properly done studies, they showed that it is no better than placebo. Large studies, they show no benefit of the thing. So then, how are we going to be-- after the properly done study showed that there is no benefit, how are we going to be telling people, oh, yeah, the thing doesn't work, but I'm still going to give it to you? So we're not in medicine giving people placebo and stuff like that, especially when it distracts from giving the proper medications.
<v Manny Gordillo>所以对我来说，伊维菌素只是一个符号。它是一个词。当他们进行妥善的研究时，他们表明它并不比安慰剂好。大规模研究表明这种东西没有好处。那么，在我们已经进行了妥善的研究证明它没有好处之后，我们怎么能告诉人们，哦，是的，那东西没用，但我还是要给你用？我们在医学上不会给人们用安慰剂之类的东西，尤其是在它分散了给予适当药物注意力的情况下。

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:27.060
<v Zoe Chace>That's what drives him nuts, when people who have been lied to refuse remdesivir, which can work, and want ivermectin, which does not.
<v Zoe Chace>这让他抓狂，当那些被欺骗的人拒绝可以起作用的瑞德西韦，却想要不起作用的伊维菌素时。

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:33.280
<v Manny Gordillo>Which just make no sense. So then it becomes a problem.
<v Manny Gordillo>这根本没道理。所以这就成了一个问题。

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:54.510
<v Zoe Chace>This is the unbridgeable divide between the medical professionals and the medical freedom people in this case. To the activists, it's bad medicine to not give ivermectin. To the professionals, it's bad medicine to give it. There's not a middle ground to be had.
<v Zoe Chace>这是医疗专业人员和医疗自由人士在此案中无法逾越的鸿沟。对于活动人士来说，不给伊维菌素是糟糕的医疗手段。对于专业人士来说，给它才是糟糕的医疗手段。没有中间立场可言。

00:27:54.510 --> 00:28:15.700
<v Zoe Chace>Dr. Gordillo is an infectious disease specialist, and he's had many of these confrontations-- conversations-- with family members demanding alternative COVID treatments over the past few years. It was rough running the hospital's COVID response. He'd coped with death threats against his family, protests outside the ICU. He'd work 100 hours a week. It was hard on his marriage.
<v Zoe Chace>戈迪略医生是一位传染病专家，在过去几年里，他与许多要求替代新冠治疗方案的家属有过多次这种对峙——对话。负责医院的新冠应对工作非常艰难。他曾面临对他家人的死亡威胁，ICU 外的抗议。他每周工作 100 小时。这对他婚姻生活影响很大。

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:24.540
<v Zoe Chace>His wife was like, these patients hate you, and you're still going to work for them? What kind of a person are you? I thought what happened at the meeting might make him mad.
<v Zoe Chace>他妻子当时说，这些病人讨厌你，而你还在为他们工作？你是什么样的人？我以为会议上发生的事情会让他生气。

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:48.010
<v Manny Gordillo>I mean, I was frustrated more than mad because by that time, I knew what the strategy of these people was. I was mad at the people that did the manipulation. I wasn't mad at the patients. The patients, in the end, they are victims from the manipulators.
<v Manny Gordillo>我的意思是，比起生气，我更多的是感到沮丧，因为那时我已经知道这些人的策略是什么了。我生那些操纵者的人的气。我不生病人的气。病人在最后是那些操纵者的受害者。

00:28:48.010 --> 00:29:12.190
<v Zoe Chace>Dr. Gordillo is from Peru, from a place he says the choices were becoming a fisherman or a priest. He tried something else, of course, but he still fishes. That's one of the things he loves about living in Sarasota. Something that surprised me, Dr. Gordillo did take a serious look at ivermectin. There were early studies showing it killed COVID in a test tube. Problem was, the doses it took were much too high to give to a human being.
<v Zoe Chace>戈迪略医生来自秘鲁，他说是那个选择要么成为渔夫要么成为牧师的地方。当然，他尝试了别的东西，但他仍然钓鱼。这是他喜欢住在萨拉索塔的原因之一。让我惊讶的一点是，戈迪略医生确实认真研究过伊维菌素。早期的研究表明它在试管中能杀死新冠病毒。问题是，人体所需的剂量太高了，无法给人类使用。

00:29:12.190 --> 00:29:33.940
<v Zoe Chace>He says, when people know they're going to die, people always ask for alternative treatments to all kinds of things, for HIV, cancer. He's seen it a lot over 30 years. And his attitude is, if the treatment won't hurt you, sure, go get it elsewhere. If an alternative clinic gives out ivermectin, he's like, whatever, they're just not going to do it at his hospital.
<v Zoe Chace>他说，当人们知道自己要死时，人们总是要求各种东西的替代疗法，艾滋病、癌症。他在 30 年里见过很多。他的态度是，如果治疗不会伤害你，当然，去别处弄吧。如果一个替代诊所发放伊维菌素，他会说，随便吧，只是他们不会在他的医院里这么做。

00:29:33.940 --> 00:30:04.100
<v Manny Gordillo>I personally don't have a problem with that. If people, in the end, they're going to decide on their own what they want to do, all I can do is give them advice on what is the best treatment as I know it, as our current state of science and the current state of knowledge tells us. And that's the way science goes.
<v Manny Gordillo>我个人对此没有意见。如果人们最终决定自己想做什么，我所能做的就是根据我所知、根据我们目前的科学状况和知识状况告诉他们什么是最好的治疗方法。这就是科学发展的道路。

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:08.390
<v Zoe Chace>I let Tanya and Vic know Gordillo had taken a serious look at ivermectin.
<v Zoe Chace>我让坦尼娅和维克知道戈迪略认真研究过伊维菌素。

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:20.030
<v Zoe Chace>But when he did look at the studies that were done in America with thousands of people, it showed that basically ivermectin was a placebo. It was like no better than a sugar pill.
<v Zoe Chace>但当他查看在美国数千人中进行的研究时，结果显示基本上伊维菌素就是安慰剂。它就像糖丸一样，没有好一点。

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:21.290
<v Vic Mellor>Can I answer this one?
<v Vic Mellor>我能回答这个吗？

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:23.000
<v Zoe Chace>Sure.
<v Zoe Chace>当然。

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:34.790
<v Vic Mellor>OK, so I would like to see this study that he's referring to, and I would like to see who did it. I'm sure it was cherry-picked because they knew this was coming up anyways.
<v Vic Mellor>好的，我想看看他提到的这项研究，我想看看是谁做的。我敢肯定它是精心挑选的，因为他们知道这件事无论如何都会被提出来。

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:43.890
<v Zoe Chace>His answer probably shouldn't surprise anyone who's been living in America lately. You have studies? I have studies too. You have doctors? I have doctors that say the opposite.
<v Zoe Chace>他的回答可能不会让任何最近住在美国的人感到惊讶。你有研究？我也有研究。你有医生？我有持相反意见的医生。

00:30:43.890 --> 00:31:02.680
<v Zoe Chace>It's not just him. He's a stand-in for thousands of epidemiologists. Like, is it possible that you guys, for all your best intentions, that you guys aren't properly evaluating scientific studies because you're not doctors?
<v Zoe Chace>不仅仅是他。他代表了成千上万的流行病学家。比如，有没有可能，虽然你们出发点是好的，但你们并没有正确评估科学研究，因为你们不是医生？

00:31:02.680 --> 00:31:28.450
<v Vic Mellor>No, I'm not a doctor. I've taken ivermectin. I know it works. I don't need a doctor to tell me it works. I know it works. I've seen other people take it who were sick-- really sick-- take it, and it works. So if he has the stance that it's still a placebo, then he's an idiot, and he's part of what's wrong with what's going on here.
<v Vic Mellor>不，我不是医生。我吃过伊维菌素。我知道它有效。我不需要医生告诉我它是否有效。我知道它有效。我看到其他人病得很重——真的很重——服用了它，它奏效了。所以如果他坚持认为它仍然是安慰剂，那么他就是个白痴，而且他是这里发生错误的一部分。

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:48.160
<v Tanya Parus>Also, Zoe, I wanted to add that I've never once said that I was the reviewer of all these clinical studies. These studies that have come out, I've personally spoken with doctors who've been evaluating the studies. And that's where I'm getting my answers from.
<v Tanya Parus>另外，佐伊，我想补充一点，我从来没说过我是所有这些临床研究的审评员。这些发布的研究，我亲自和评估过这些研究的医生谈过。这就是我得到答案的地方。

00:31:48.160 --> 00:32:02.990
<v Zoe Chace>The two sides on this are so divided and both so embattled that the one thing they have in common is how misunderstood each side feels by the other. At that board meeting at Sarasota Memorial Hospital, it was true for Dr. Gordillo. It was true for Tanya.
<v Zoe Chace>这两方对此的分歧如此之大，且都如此深陷其中，他们唯一的共同点是双方都觉得对方是多么地误解自己。在萨拉索塔纪念医院的那次董事会会议上，对于戈迪略医生来说是这样。对于坦尼娅来说也是这样。

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:26.930
<v Tanya Parus>I don't think you'd understand unless you were on this side of the opinion gateway how shitty they made us feel. Because of what? To me, it's like, why am I-- I just don't believe in mandates. It doesn't mean that I'm a fascist. It doesn't mean--
<v Tanya Parus>我想除非你站在观点闸门的这一边，否则你不会明白他们让我们感觉有多糟糕。因为什么？对我来说，就像是，为什么我——我只是不相信强制令。这并不意味着我是法西斯。这并不意味着——

00:32:26.930 --> 00:32:31.010
<v Zoe Chace>Tanya was like, OK, it's not going to work at Sarasota Memorial.
<v Zoe Chace>坦尼娅觉得，好吧，萨拉索塔纪念医院行不通。

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:33.770
<v Tanya Parus>The hospital would never surrender.
<v Tanya Parus>医院绝不会投降。

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:35.090
<v Zoe Chace>The hospital was not going to change?
<v Zoe Chace>医院不会改变？

00:32:35.090 --> 00:32:57.350
<v Tanya Parus>No, they were never even going to offer an apology. So I knew that there was-- we needed a place where people could go and be seen by a doctor, regardless. So let's say another pandemic happens. Are you still comfortable going to see your doctor today? No. Will you be in two years if another pandemic happens? No.
<v Tanya Parus>不，他们甚至从未打算道歉。所以我知道——我们需要一个地方，无论如何，人们可以去那里并被医生看诊。所以，假设另一场大流行发生了。你今天还愿意去看你的医生吗？不。如果在两年内另一场大流行发生，你还会去吗？不。

00:32:57.350 --> 00:33:08.570
<v Tanya Parus>So we still need a place. And that was the whole point with Vic was, I just, more so than anything, told him, we've got to get something. We have to get something up. We have to.
<v Tanya Parus>所以我们仍然需要一个地方。这就是维克所关心的核心，我只是，比什么都重要，告诉他，我们必须弄到点东西。我们必须建立起什么东西。我们必须。

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:09.470
<v Zoe Chace>After that meeting?
<v Zoe Chace>在那次会议之后？

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:10.340
<v Tanya Parus>Yeah.
<v Tanya Parus>是的。

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:41.550
<v Zoe Chace>That's when the Medical Center at 959 East Venice Avenue became a reality. Florida's a great place to do this. Tanya's side is winning in Florida. In September 2021, DeSantis hired this vaccine-skeptical surgeon general to run Florida's response to the COVID pandemic, someone who altered a report's findings to claim the COVID vaccine had more adverse side effects than it actually has.
<v Zoe Chace>那时候东威尼斯大道 959 号的医疗中心才成为现实。佛罗里达州是做这件事的好地方。坦尼娅这边在佛罗里达州赢了。2021 年 9 月，德桑蒂斯聘请了一位对疫苗持怀疑态度的外科医生将军来负责佛罗里达州的新冠大流行应对工作，此人篡改了一份报告的结论，声称新冠疫苗的不良副作用比实际情况要多。

00:33:41.550 --> 00:34:02.160
<v Zoe Chace>DeSantis started publicly questioning the vaccines, saying they could be dangerous, and publicly deriding CDC protocols. This anti-vaccine agenda in Florida arguably had consequences. One study estimated 30,000 extra deaths just in Florida because people didn't get vaccinated.
<v Zoe Chace>德桑蒂斯开始公开质疑疫苗，称它们可能很危险，并公开嘲讽 CDC 的规程。佛罗里达州的这种反疫苗议程可以说是有后果的。一项研究估计仅在佛罗里达州就造成了 3 万例额外死亡，因为人们没有接种疫苗。

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:34.650
<v Zoe Chace>DeSantis also created this brand-new committee of physicians who would assess any federal health guidelines coming into the state. Dr. Gordillo at Sarasota Memorial Hospital calls this group radical, extremely right wing, outside of mainstream science. The same day he announced that committee, DeSantis filed a petition with the Florida Supreme Court to convene a grand jury, a grand jury to look into things Pfizer and Moderna claimed about the vaccine, which he says were criminally misleading. Dr. Gordillo was like, what is happening?
<v Zoe Chace>德桑蒂斯还创建了一个全新的内科医生委员会，他们将评估进入该州的任何联邦健康指南。萨拉索塔纪念医院的戈迪略医生称这个团体为激进、极右翼，背离主流科学。在他宣布那个委员会的同一天，德桑蒂斯向佛罗里达州最高法院提交了一份请愿书，要求召集大陪审团，大陪审团调查辉瑞和 Moderna 关于疫苗的声明，他说这些声明具有严重的误导性。戈迪略医生当时想，发生了什么？

00:34:34.650 --> 00:34:53.670
<v Manny Gordillo>It's just, the whole thing to me is just so politicized. And that upsets me because to see that coming from our governor, that's a little bit too much for me.
<v Manny Gordillo>对我来说，这一切都太政治化了。这让我感到沮丧，因为看到那是来自我们的州长，对我来说有点过头了。

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:55.890
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, what do you mean, too much for you?
<v Zoe Chace>是的，你说的“过头了”是什么意思？

00:34:55.890 --> 00:35:12.390
<v Manny Gordillo>Yeah, I can't take it. It's just-- [LAUGHS] Yeah, it's something that I never thought that it was going to happen in the United States. Maybe if I'm living in North Korea, perhaps, or in China, but not in the US.
<v Manny Gordillo>是的，我受不了了。这只是——[笑声] 是的，这是我想象中在美国永远不会发生的事情。也许如果我生活在朝鲜，或者在中国，可能，但不是在美国。

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:13.770
<v Zoe Chace>In what sense?
<v Zoe Chace>在什么意义上？

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:32.200
<v Manny Gordillo>In the sense that we're telling people-- we're getting a radical view, and do not accept any other points of view, and then push the agenda.
<v Manny Gordillo>在某种意义上，我们告诉人们——我们正在获得一种激进的观点，并且不接受任何其他观点，然后推行这个议程。

00:35:32.200 --> 00:35:48.190
<v Zoe Chace>DeSantis is now campaigning for president on his COVID policies, the way he, quote unquote, "fought back the Iron Curtain of Faucism." The language he uses seems designed to appeal to anyone who believes that COVID was a virus created to control us all.
<v Zoe Chace>德桑蒂斯现在正以他的新冠政策竞选总统，他是如何，引用他的话说，“击退了福奇主义（Faucism）的铁幕”。他使用的语言似乎旨在吸引任何相信新冠是一种旨在控制我们所有人的病毒的人。

00:35:48.190 --> 00:35:58.360
<v Ron DeSantis>From the very beginning, we refused to let this state descend into some type of Faucian dystopia.
<v Ron DeSantis>从一开始，我们就拒绝让这个州陷入某种福奇式的反乌托邦。

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:00.730
<v Zoe Chace>This is him at CPAC a year ago.
<v Zoe Chace>这是他一年前在 CPAC 会议上说的。

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:24.670
<v Ron DeSantis>In Florida, we reject the biomedical security state which erodes liberty, harms livelihoods, and divides our society. We've stood for freedom across the board, and the result has been, Florida has defeated Faucism. Freedom has prevailed in the Sunshine State.
<v Ron DeSantis>在佛罗里达州，我们拒绝侵蚀自由、损害生计并分裂我们社会的生物医学安全国家。我们全面维护自由，结果就是，佛罗里达州击败了福奇主义。自由在阳光之州取得了胜利。

00:36:24.670 --> 00:36:49.760
<v Zoe Chace>Governor DeSantis just signed a set of four medical freedom bills into law two months ago. The press release reads, "Permanent protections for Floridians from the biomedical security state." It not only outlaws any, quote, "discrimination based on vaccine status," it also protects a doctor's right to disagree with what DeSantis has called, "the preferred narrative of the medical community."
<v Zoe Chace>德桑蒂斯州长两个月前刚签署了一套四项医疗自由法案。新闻稿写道：“佛罗里达人免受生物医学安全国家侵害的永久保护。”它不仅禁止任何所谓的“基于疫苗状况的歧视”，它还保护了医生不同意德桑蒂斯所称的“医疗界首选叙事”的权利。

00:36:49.760 --> 00:37:07.700
<v Zoe Chace>The legislation protects doctors from any discipline by state medical boards for spreading wrong information about, say, ivermectin or the vaccine. That's good news for Vic's clinic and any doctors or nurses who choose to work there. DeSantis wants them to keep practicing here in Florida.
<v Zoe Chace>该立法保护医生免受州医疗委员会因传播关于——比如——伊维菌素或疫苗的错误信息而进行的任何处罚。这对维克的诊所和任何选择在那里工作的医生或护士来说都是好消息。德桑蒂斯希望他们继续在佛罗里达州执业。

00:37:07.700 --> 00:37:44.390
<v Zoe Chace>There have been a bunch of other medical freedom-type bills introduced in state legislatures around the country in the past year. And you can see some Republican legislators citing what's happening in Florida as their inspiration. Some of these bills protect doctors who want to promote things like ivermectin for COVID. Florida's law is far and away the most comprehensive. It's the only one that prohibits, quote, "international health organizations"-- the ones Vic and Tanya are so worried about-- "from dictating state policy," for example. Florida stands alone.
<v Zoe Chace>过去一年里，全国各地的州议会提出了许多其他医疗自由类型的法案。你可以看到一些共和党议员引用佛罗里达州正在发生的事情作为他们的灵感。其中一些法案保护那些想要为新冠推广伊维菌素等药物的医生。佛罗里达州的法律是目前为止最全面的。它是唯一禁止——比如——“国际卫生组织”——维克和坦尼娅如此担心的那些组织——“支配州政策”的法律。佛罗里达州独树一帜。

00:37:44.390 --> 00:38:04.940
<v Zoe Chace>As Vic prepares for his clinic's opening in September, he's part of a growing market for anti-vax enthusiasts. The medical freedom movement already has blood banks for the unvaccinated, a whole suite of unvaccinated fertility options, unvaccinated sperm, unvaccinated surrogacy, unvaccinated eggs, unvaccinated breast milk donation.
<v Zoe Chace>当维克准备在九月开业诊所时，他也是反疫苗爱好者日益增长的市场的一部分。医疗自由运动已经有了为未接种疫苗者提供的血库、一整套未接种疫苗的生育选择、未接种疫苗的精子、未接种疫苗的代孕、未接种疫苗的卵子、未接种疫苗的母乳捐赠。

