00:41:01.330 --> 00:41:16.020
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show, "The Florida Experiment." Ron DeSantis wants to make America more like Florida. We go for a visit to see what that might be like. We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act two, Their Eyes Were Watching Tallahassee.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，“佛罗里达实验”。罗恩·德桑蒂斯想让美国变得更像佛罗里达。我们去参观一下，看看那会是什么样子。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕，他们的眼睛盯着塔拉哈西（Tallahassee）。

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:29.460
<v Ira Glass>So probably the biggest part of Governor DeSantis' agenda focuses on education. He and Florida Republicans have passed law after law defining what can and cannot be taught in the classroom. And that's not just in elementary and high schools. It's also in universities.
<v Ira Glass>所以德桑蒂斯州长议程中可能最重要的部分集中在教育上。他和佛罗里达州的共和党人通过了一项又一项法律，规定了课堂上能教什么和不能教什么。而且这不仅仅是在小学和高中。这也适用于大学。

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:57.700
<v Ira Glass>This February, Florida Republicans in the statehouse introduced this big bill, HB 999 to limit what you can teach in every public college and university in the state. The earliest versions of the bill were far-reaching. They didn't just ban critical race theory and identity politics from all general education courses. They made it so no student could major or minor in gender studies or in what the bill calls "radical feminist theory," things that have been standard in universities for decades.
<v Ira Glass>今年 2 月，佛罗里达州议会的共和党人提出了这项大法案，HB 999，限制你可以在该州每一所公立学院和大学教什么。该法案的早期版本影响深远。他们不仅在所有通识教育课程中禁止了批判性种族理论和身份政治。他们还规定，任何学生都不得主修或辅修性别研究，或者该法案所称的“激进女性主义理论”，这些几十年来在大学里一直是标准的学科。

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:13.660
<v Ira Glass>For professors who might try to sneak something by, early drafts of the bill didn't explicitly say what would happen to them. But they did say that tenured professors could have their tenure reviewed at any time. Understandably, professors all over the state wondered what this new bill was going to mean in practice.
<v Ira Glass>对于那些可能试图偷偷教点什么的教授，法案的早期草案没有明确说明会发生什么。但他们确实说，终身教授的终身职位可以随时被审查。可以理解，全州的教授们都在想，这项新法案在实践中到底意味着什么。

00:42:13.660 --> 00:42:29.750
<v Ira Glass>Emmanuel Dzotsi wondered that too and followed things for one semester between the introduction of the bill and its final passage at one of the biggest universities in Florida, Florida State. He has this story about what it was like for a professor and one of her students.
<v Ira Glass>伊曼纽尔·佐齐（Emmanuel Dzotsi）也对此感到好奇，并在法案提出到最终通过之间的一个学期里，跟踪了佛罗里达州最大的大学之一——佛罗里达州立大学（Florida State University）的情况。他有这个关于一位教授和她的一个学生的故事。

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:50.640
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>So one of the many students affected by this new law is Kaysin. She's Black, moved to Florida as a kid. She was a freshman this past year. Kaysin's very bright, and not just in the smart and intelligent kind of way. She's a really positive person, very earnest. At a school with more than 30,000 undergrads, she couldn't meet enough of them.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>所以受这项新法律影响的众多学生之一是凯辛（Kaysin）。她是黑人，小时候搬到了佛罗里达州。去年她是新生。凯辛非常聪明，而且不仅仅是那种机灵和智能意义上的。她是一个非常积极、非常真诚的人。在一所拥有超过 3 万名本科生的学校里，她见不完这么多人。

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:59.270
<v Kaysin>I will start talking to people if we just happen to be passing each other and I notice something. Like, oh, my gosh, your hair looks so cute.
<v Kaysin>如果我们刚好擦肩而过，我注意到什么，我就会开始和人聊天。比如，哦，我的天，你的头发看起来真可爱。

00:42:59.270 --> 00:43:01.540
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Wow. You're a compliment queen.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>哇。你真是个“赞美女王”。

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:09.470
<v Kaysin>[LAUGHS] Yes, that's probably a good way to put it. I meet a lot of people that way.
<v Kaysin>[笑声] 是的，这可能是一个好的说法。我就是那样认识很多人的。

00:43:09.470 --> 00:43:28.490
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Kaysin was also super passionate about the academic side of things. She chose Florida State, in part, because she got a scholarship, and she was aiming to be the first person in her family to get a bachelor's degree. Her focus was to do something that would get her a job, so she looked into picking up a major in entrepreneurship, which would give her the tools to start her own business.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>凯辛对学术方面也充满热情。她选择佛罗里达州立大学的部分原因是她获得了奖学金，而且她的目标是成为家里第一个获得学士学位的人。她的重点是做一些能让她找到工作的事情，所以她研究了修读创业专业，这将给她提供开创自己企业的工具。

00:43:28.490 --> 00:43:46.100
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But then she signed up for this African-American studies class, just, you know, to try it, which, as any disappointed parent of a business major who drops it all for a life in humanities can tell you, this is how it starts. Kaysin loved the course, couldn't ask enough questions of her professor.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但后来她注册了这门非裔美国人研究课程，只是，你知道，试一试，这正如任何一个对放弃一切去投身人文科学而感到失望的商科学生家长可以告诉你的那样，这就是开始。凯辛喜欢这门课程，对她的教授问不够问题。

00:43:46.100 --> 00:44:09.650
<v Kaysin>I lost my mind. I entered a fervor. Because I knew I could not ask him every single question I wanted to in the middle of class, I would literally write down every single one of my thoughts. And then after class, I would literally follow him out of the building. We would just talk for as he's walking to his car from the classroom.
<v Kaysin>我简直疯了。我进入了一种狂热状态。因为我知道我不能在课间问他我想问的每一个问题，我简直会写下我所有的想法。然后下课后，我会跟着他走出大楼。就在他从教室走到车边的路上，我们只是在交谈。

00:44:09.650 --> 00:44:11.040
<v Kaysin>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kaysin>[笑声]

00:44:11.040 --> 00:44:14.160
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>So this man is trying to leave, and you're just like, could you--
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>所以这个人正试图离开，你就像是，你能——

00:44:14.160 --> 00:44:21.450
<v Kaysin>Yes. I would just be talking to him and asking him more and more questions about what he thought about different things.
<v Kaysin>是的。我只是会和他说话，并问他更多关于他对自己所想的不同事物的看法。

00:44:21.450 --> 00:44:49.680
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Kaysin loved the class so much that by the end of the semester, she switched her major to English and added a minor in African-American studies. She wanted to dive deeper on Black writers from her state, from Florida. So for her second semester, she signed up for a class on Southern Black literature, the Literatures and Cultures of the Afro Gulf South, taught by a Black female professor named Dr. Alisha Gaines. And when Kaysin met Dr. Gaines on the first day of class, she was obsessed with her.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>凯辛非常喜欢这门课，到学期结束时，她把专业换成了英语，并增加了非裔美国人研究辅修。她想深入研究她所在州——佛罗里达州——的黑人作家。所以第二个学期，她注册了一门关于南方黑人文学的课，即“非裔海湾南方文学与文化”，由一位名叫艾丽莎·盖恩斯（Alisha Gaines）博士的黑人女教授授课。当凯辛第一天上课见到盖恩斯博士时，她就被她迷住了。

00:44:49.680 --> 00:45:07.800
<v Kaysin>I thought she was so cool. [LAUGHS] She just arrived into class with this kind of warm confidence. I remember noticing that she was wearing what I would say is kind of almost this fancy black dress, but then was wearing sneakers with it.
<v Kaysin>我觉得她太酷了。[笑声] 她带着一种温暖的自信走进教室。我记得注意到她穿的是那种我称之为几乎是华丽的黑色连衣裙，但脚下穿的是运动鞋。

00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:09.270
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>The sneakers got you?
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>运动鞋打动了你？

00:45:09.270 --> 00:45:32.820
<v Kaysin>I don't know. It was my first time having a Black professor in college, a Black female professor in college. And it was just-- it was like I was able to look at her and think to myself, I could be like that someday. That's something I could be.
<v Kaysin>我不知道。这是我第一次在大学里有一位黑人教授，一位黑人女教授。这就像——我能够看着她并心想，总有一天我也能像那样。那是我可以成为的样子。

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:49.570
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Within a few weeks of meeting Dr. Gaines, Kaysin decided she was going to become just like her, become a professor of African-American literature. But right as she figured out what she wanted to do with her life, HB 999 was introduced. It was late February, a few weeks into the semester.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>遇见盖恩斯博士几周后，凯辛决定她要变得像她一样，成为一名非裔美国文学教授。但就在她弄清楚了自己想做什么时，HB 999 法案被提出了。那是 2 月下旬，学期进行了几周。

00:45:49.570 --> 00:46:01.020
<v Kaysin>I think that I first saw it on Instagram. And I read over it. And I thought to myself, well, that can't be true. That's insane.
<v Kaysin>我想我第一次是在 Instagram 上看到的。我读了一遍。我心想，这不可能是真的。这太疯狂了。

00:46:01.020 --> 00:46:24.360
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Kaysin realized that a lot of the things that she loved most about Gaines' class might be affected by this bill. The class was, of course, about literature. But it also involved discussions about the cultural and political context for each book. For example, at one point, they read a novel about one family's experience of Hurricane Katrina, and they talked about the role systemic racism played in the government's response to it.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>凯辛意识到，她最喜欢盖恩斯课程的许多地方可能会受到这项法案的影响。这门课当然是关于文学的。但它也涉及关于每本书的文化和政治背景的讨论。例如，有一次，他们读了一本关于一个家庭经历卡特里娜飓风的小说，他们谈论了系统性种族主义在政府应对中的作用。

00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:47.840
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>It felt like without those sorts of discussions, Literatures and Cultures of the Afro Gulf South just wasn't Literatures and Cultures of the Afro Gulf South. And she felt like her entire field of study, Black literature, specifically Black literature that probed the true legacy of what it means to be Black in America, the thing she had just realized she wanted to make her life's work, was under threat.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>感觉如果没有那些讨论，“非裔海湾南方文学与文化”就不再是“非裔海湾南方文学与文化”了。她觉得她整个研究领域，即黑人文学，特别是那些探究作为美国黑人真正意味着什么的黑人文学，也就是她刚刚意识到她想作为毕生事业去做的事情，正受到威胁。

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:56.090
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Like, there was one day where they were discussing Their Eyes Were Watching God in class, and Kaysin just couldn't stop thinking about the bill.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>就像，有一天他们在课堂上讨论《他们的眼睛望着上帝》（Their Eyes Were Watching God），凯辛就是无法停止思考这项法案。

00:46:56.090 --> 00:47:15.720
<v Kaysin>I was just thinking about, what if no one else ever gets to even read this in a class again? What if no one gets to have these kind of discussions that we're having again?
<v Kaysin>我只是在想，如果以后再也没有人能在课堂上读到这个怎么办？如果以后再也没有人能像我们这样讨论怎么办？

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:38.350
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Kaysin wondered how seriously she should take the bill. It was something I was wondering about too. I thought, this bill is just a draft. Surely a lot of this sweeping language targeting subjects, that was going to come out, right? And even if it didn't and the bill passed, it's such an attack on free speech, surely it wouldn't hold up in court.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>凯辛想知道她应该多认真地对待这项法案。这也是我一直在想的。我想，这项法案只是个草案。肯定很多这种针对学科的宽泛语言会被删掉，对吧？即使没有被删掉且法案通过了，这也是对言论自由的攻击，肯定在法庭上站不住脚。

00:47:38.350 --> 00:48:01.830
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But pretty much every one of the professors who talked to me at FSU said that the bill's very introduction had a chilling effect. The language in the bill was so broad, it was hard to pin down exactly what would be prohibited. Professors told me that they and their colleagues were feeling pressured to change things about their work, regardless of what happened with the law. And Dr. Gaines says, that didn't seem like an accident.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但几乎每一个和我谈过的佛罗里达州立大学的教授都说，该法案的提出本身就产生了“寒蝉效应”。法案中的语言如此宽泛，很难确定到底什么会被禁止。教授们告诉我，无论法律最后如何，他们和他们的同事都感到有压力要改变他们的工作内容。盖恩斯博士说，这看起来不像是个意外。

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:16.200
<v Alisha Gaines>Some of the early versions of this legislation is so ridiculous and outlandish. And you realize, it's written to chill. It's written to put us on notice.
<v Alisha Gaines>这项立法的一些早期版本是如此荒谬和古怪。你意识到，它是为了制造寒蝉效应而写的。它是为了让我们收到通知而写的。

00:48:16.200 --> 00:48:39.340
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But even believing that, it was hard to know what to do. Like, the same week the bill was introduced, Dr. Gaines got word that she was going to be teaching a course she hadn't taught before in the fall, Major Figures in American Literature, which is a really broad subject, right? In theory, you could teach any assortment of great American authors. And Dr. Gaines was considering making the focus of the class the authors of the Harlem Renaissance.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但即使相信这一点，也很难知道该怎么办。就像法案提出的同一周，盖恩斯博士接到通知，她秋季要教一门她以前没教过的课程：“美国文学中的主要人物”，这真是一个宽泛的主题，对吧？理论上，你可以教任何伟大的美国作家组合。盖恩斯博士正在考虑将课程重点放在哈莱姆文艺复兴时期的作家身上。

00:48:39.340 --> 00:48:57.250
<v Alisha Gaines>The authors that I would want to talk with students about are major American authors, but they're also Black. So can I just teach Black folks, and people will be fine with that? Or are they wanting Whitman? Or are they wanting Faulkner?
<v Alisha Gaines>我想和学生讨论的作家是主要的美国作家，但他们也是黑人。所以我能只教黑人吗，大家会觉得没问题吗？还是他们想要惠特曼（Whitman）？或者是他们想要福克纳（Faulkner）？

00:48:57.250 --> 00:49:14.970
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Gaines wondered, with HB 999 and all of the discourse around education in the state, if she could teach the historical racial context around an era like the Harlem Renaissance and its continued relevance today in a class where some students would just be there to fulfill a writing requirement.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>盖恩斯想，在 HB 999 法案和该州关于教育的所有讨论下，她是否能在这样一个班级里教授像哈莱姆文艺复兴时期这样的历史背景及其今天持续的相关性，而班里有些学生只是为了满足写作要求才来上课。

00:49:14.970 --> 00:49:26.740
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>She would encounter students from all different kinds of majors, students who might not necessarily be trying to learn about Black writers, and who would be able to record her classes and report her.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>她会遇到来自各种各样专业的学生，有些学生可能不一定试图学习黑人作家，而且他们能录下她的课并举报她。

00:49:26.740 --> 00:50:01.240
<v Alisha Gaines>Is there going to be a student in my class taking it just to be controversial, just to be difficult? That kind of stuff you don't want to keep at the forefront of your brain when you're trying to interact with students, and learn with them, and engage the material. But you have to think about it a little bit. And then it creates a little bit of a, can I-- can I fully trust this classroom? Can I fully trust all my students?
<v Alisha Gaines>会不会有一个学生为了争议、为了制造困难来选我的课？当你试图和学生互动、学习、参与材料时，你不想把那种东西放在大脑的前沿。但你不得不考虑一点。然后这创造了一种，我可以——我可以完全信任这个教室吗？我可以完全信任我所有的学生吗？

00:50:01.240 --> 00:50:22.350
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>In March, I heard about something that was adding to the sense of dread so many people I talked to were feeling. It was a list that was getting passed around, and on it were 34 classes at FSU. Most of the professors I talked to about it didn't know exactly what it meant to be on the list, but they figured it wasn't a good thing.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>三月，我听说了一些事情，这增加了许多和我谈过的人感到的恐惧感。那是一份被传阅的清单，上面有佛罗里达州立大学的 34 门课程。大多数和我讨论过它的教授不知道被列入清单到底意味着什么，但他们觉得这不是什么好事。

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:29.490
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>At first, it wasn't exactly clear who created the list. The DeSantis administration? The university?
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>起初，还不清楚是谁创建了这份清单。德桑蒂斯政府？还是大学？

00:50:29.490 --> 00:50:32.310
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>So the first class on the list is--
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>所以清单上的第一门课是——

00:50:32.310 --> 00:50:52.020
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Gaines and I looked through the list. There are programs and classes you'd expect to see, courses that talk about race and gender in ways that might concern Republicans on an anti-woke crusade, like AMH 2096, Black Women in America, or SYD 3800, Sociology of Sex and Gender.
<v Ira Glass>盖恩斯博士和我查看了这份清单。有你预料中会看到的项目和课程，那些以共和党人在“反觉醒”十字军东征中可能关心的那种方式讨论种族和性别的课程，比如 AMH 2096，“美国的黑人女性”，或 SYD 3800，“性别社会学”。

00:50:52.020 --> 00:51:04.710
<v Ira Glass>But there are many classes missing. Dr. Gaines' class that Kaysin was in, Literatures and Cultures of the Afro Gulf South, wasn't on there, which made no sense, not that she was complaining.
<v Ira Glass>但有很多课程没在上面。凯辛上过的那门盖恩斯博士的“非裔海湾南方文学与文化”没在上面，这毫无道理，不是说她在抱怨。

00:51:04.710 --> 00:51:14.080
<v Alisha Gaines>That-- excuse me-- legislators, that is an exhaustive list of all the courses that you need to worry about. Thank you. Complete list, don't look any further.
<v Alisha Gaines>那个——抱歉——立法者们，那是你们需要担心的所有课程的详尽列表。谢谢。完整的列表，不要再看了。

00:51:14.080 --> 00:51:16.030
<v Alisha Gaines>[LAUGHTER]
<v Alisha Gaines>[笑声]

00:51:16.030 --> 00:51:40.650
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>You also had a lot of classes that didn't seem to fit, like Classical Perspectives on Dance, Masterpieces of German Literature. After looking into it, I figured out that the university made the list. The DeSantis administration had requested all public universities send in a comprehensive list of anything on campus related to diversity and inclusion and critical race theory.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>你还有很多看起来并不合适的课程，比如“舞蹈的古典视角”、“德国文学名著”。深入调查后，我发现是大学制作了这份清单。德桑蒂斯政府要求所有公立大学提交一份校园内关于多元化、包容性和批判性种族理论的所有东西的详尽清单。

00:51:40.650 --> 00:52:03.360
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I couldn't get anyone at the school to explain to me why this oddly random list of courses was what they decided to send in. Was it some Machiavellian mind game? Was it incompetence? Who knows? But this disembodied list being passed around, it had an effect. It added to the web of self-doubt and fear that professors at FSU were feeling.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>学校里没有人能向我解释为什么这份奇怪的随机课程列表是他们决定提交的东西。这是一场马基雅维利式的心理游戏吗？是无能吗？谁知道呢？但这份被传阅的游离清单确实产生了影响。它增加了佛罗里达州立大学教授们感受到的自我怀疑和恐惧的网络。

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:18.930
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Some of them were already changing their courses, trying to make them hold up to scrutiny. I talked to one professor who removed work by Ibram Kendi from his syllabus and emphasized empirical studies about traffic stops and hiring discrimination instead.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>他们中有些人已经在改变课程，试图让它们经得起审查。我和一位教授谈过，他从教学大纲中删除了伊布拉姆·肯迪（Ibram Kendi）的作品，并强调了关于交通拦截和招聘歧视的实证研究。

00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:48.430
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Two other professors took the understandable step of changing the names of their courses, basically erasing the giant targets they had on their backs. A class called "Critical Race Theory" will now be listed as "The Sociology of Race and Ethnicity." Another class, "Feminism and Travel," you can find out under the new name, "Journeys in Women's Literature." Both courses are, content-wise, basically the same as they were before.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>另外两名教授采取了可以理解的步骤，改变了课程名称，基本上擦掉了他们背上的巨大靶子。一门名为“批判性种族理论”的课程现在将被列为“种族与族裔社会学”。另一门课程，“女性主义与旅行”，你可以在新名字“女性文学中的旅程”下找到它。内容上，这两门课程与以前基本相同。

00:52:48.430 --> 00:52:56.170
<v Celia Caputi>We are already self-censoring. And, in fact, I pulled critical race theory out of my self-description on my faculty web page.
<v Celia Caputi>我们已经在自我审查了。事实上，我从我教师网页的自我描述中删除了批判性种族理论。

00:52:56.170 --> 00:53:13.840
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>That's Dr. Celia Caputi, one of Dr. Gaines' colleagues in the English department. She teaches classes about women's literature. And the subjects targeted in the legislation were so open to interpretation with no explanation of what the punishment would be that she honestly couldn't figure out what she should be teaching.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>那是西莉亚·卡普蒂（Celia Caputi）博士，盖恩斯博士在英语系的同事。她教授关于女性文学的课程。立法中针对的主题非常容易被解读，且没有解释惩罚会是什么，以至于她真的不知道她应该教什么。

00:53:13.840 --> 00:53:27.090
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Feminist theory and gender studies were baked into her courses. She worried about losing her tenure and getting fired. And so she spent an enormous amount of time this past semester trying to do whatever she could to protect herself.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>女性主义理论和性别研究融入了她的课程。她担心失去终身职位并被解雇。所以她在过去一个学期里花了大量时间试图尽一切努力保护自己。

00:53:27.090 --> 00:53:46.540
<v Celia Caputi>I had started writing a new article just about a week ago. And now I look at the first page, and I was really proud. I wrote down-- I wrote the introduction up. I had written a lot of notes, but I finally sat down at the computer and wrote up the introduction. And, well, guess what. It references a concept called the "lesbian continuum" in the second paragraph.
<v Celia Caputi>我大约一周前刚开始写一篇文章。现在我看着第一页，我真的感到自豪。我写下——我写了介绍。我写了很多笔记，但我终于坐到电脑前写了介绍。而且，嗯，猜猜看。它在第二段提到了一个叫“女同性恋连续体”（lesbian continuum）的概念。

00:53:46.540 --> 00:53:48.020
<v Celia Caputi>[LAUGHTER]
<v Celia Caputi>[笑声]

00:53:48.020 --> 00:53:49.820
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>What's the lesbian continuum?
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>什么是女同性恋连续体？

00:53:49.820 --> 00:54:29.030
<v Celia Caputi>OK, the lesbian continuum is a concept here-- we're talking feminist theory, uh-oh! The F word, that terrible thing-- the lesbian continuum is a concept put forth by lesbian feminist poet Adrienne Rich. And her theory is that in a patriarchal heterosexist culture where women are fully responsible for caring for infants, every child, every infant's first love is his or her mother. And so that for women, the initial disposition is same-sex love, and that heterosexist culture forces a woman to realign herself. And--
<v Celia Caputi>好的，女同性恋连续体是一个概念，这里——我们谈论的是女性主义理论，噢噢！F 开头的词，那可怕的东西——女同性恋连续体是女同性恋女性主义诗人阿德里安娜·里奇（Adrienne Rich）提出的一个概念。她的理论是，在家长制异性恋中心主义文化中，女性完全负责照顾婴儿，每个孩子、每个婴儿的初恋都是他或她的母亲。所以对于女性来说，最初的倾向是同性之爱，而异性恋中心主义文化迫使女性重新调整自己。而且——

00:54:29.030 --> 00:54:38.390
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>In other words, if you're a Florida Republican who believes that radical liberal professors are turning students gay, this article would be a red flag.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>换句话说，如果你是一个佛罗里达州的共和党人，相信激进的自由派教授正在把学生变成同性恋，这篇文章会是一个危险信号。

00:54:38.390 --> 00:54:40.490
<v Celia Caputi>You see now how this puts me in this situation?
<v Celia Caputi>你现在看到这让我处于这种境地了吗？

00:54:40.490 --> 00:54:43.580
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Yeah. [LAUGHS] I see now how you're in it, yeah.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>是的。[笑声] 我现在看到你是怎么处于其中的了，是的。

00:54:43.580 --> 00:55:02.250
<v Celia Caputi>Yeah, everything I want to do, everything I can get excited about, makes me feel insecure at the same time. Is this going to make me or break me?
<v Celia Caputi>是的，我想做的每一件事，我能感到兴奋的每一件事，同时又让我感到不安。这会成就我还是毁了我？

00:55:02.250 --> 00:55:05.130
<v Alex Andrade>The chilling effect is a lie.
<v Alex Andrade>“寒蝉效应”是个谎言。

00:55:05.130 --> 00:55:24.150
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>This is one of the people who wrote HB 999, State Representative Alex Andrade. He'd been hearing about this chilling effect from so many critics of the bill. He was defensive when asked about it at first, about his intent, very angry that people thought he and the bill were racist, which he denies.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>这是 HB 999 的起草者之一，州众议员亚历克斯·安德拉德（Alex Andrade）。他从该法案的许多批评者那里听到了关于这种寒蝉效应的说法。起初被问及他的意图时，他显得很防御，非常愤怒人们认为他和这项法案是种族主义的，他对此予以否认。

00:55:24.150 --> 00:55:37.890
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>He says the fear that greeted HB 999 was inflamed by Democrats and the media. When I said that professors were feeling like they needed to change their courses, he was like, good. Maybe they need to.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>他说，迎接 HB 999 的恐惧是被民主党人和媒体煽动起来的。当我说教授们感觉需要改变课程时，他说，很好。也许他们需要这样做。

00:55:37.890 --> 00:55:53.110
<v Alex Andrade>The whole purpose of the bill is to, if you're going to make an argument, tie it to fact. Tie it to evidence. And if you want to still teach a conclusion without evidence, higher education institutions in the state of Florida may not be for you.
<v Alex Andrade>法案的全部目的是，如果你要提出一个论点，请将其与事实挂钩。将其与证据挂钩。如果你仍然想在没有证据的情况下教一个结论，佛罗里达州的高等教育机构可能不适合你。

00:55:53.110 --> 00:56:19.320
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Here's the thing. Andrade doesn't believe that systemic racism is inherent to the institutions of the United States today, even though a whole host of academic work says it is. He says, it's important that people learn about the systemic racism of the past. Learn about Jim Crow, for example. But that's the past, he says. If there's an institution that's racist right now, he said to me, I'll fight it with you.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>重点是。安德拉德不相信系统性种族主义在今天的美国制度中是固有的，尽管大量的学术著作都这么说。他说，人们了解过去系统性种族主义很重要。例如，了解吉姆·克劳法。但他说是过去的事了。他说，如果现在有一个种族主义机构，他会对我说，我会和你一起斗争。

00:56:19.320 --> 00:56:21.720
<v Alex Andrade>Tell me what institution is racist.
<v Alex Andrade>告诉我哪个机构是种族主义的。

00:56:21.720 --> 00:56:38.340
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Our justice system, it is inherently unequal and does disproportionately affect Black men. And there is a direct line between high rates of incarceration and policing that is tied to possible implicit racial bias. I think that's pretty cut and dry.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我们的司法系统，它本质上是不平等的，确实不成比例地影响着黑人男性。在极高的监禁率和警务之间有一条直接的线，这与可能的隐含种族偏见有关。我认为那相当清楚。

00:56:38.340 --> 00:56:50.070
<v Alex Andrade>Well, no, there's no question that there have been instances. What I've just told you is that if you point me to an institution that is intentionally being racist today, I will fight it with you.
<v Alex Andrade>嗯，不，毫无疑问确实存在过例子。我刚才告诉你的是，如果你指出一个今天正在蓄意种族主义的机构，我会和你一起斗争。

00:56:50.070 --> 00:56:52.540
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>OK, that's great.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>好的，那太棒了。

00:56:52.540 --> 00:57:05.350
<v Ira Glass>I hope you're noticing the distinction Andrade is making here. He's saying, there are isolated instances of, say, Black men being arrested because they're Black. But that's not proof of institutional racism.
<v Ira Glass>我希望你注意到安德拉德在这里做出的区别。他是在说，有孤立的例子，比如黑人男性因为是黑人而被捕。但这并不能证明制度性种族主义。

00:57:05.350 --> 00:57:11.440
<v Alex Andrade>There's no evidence. If you can't point to me what institution is racist today.
<v Alex Andrade>没有证据。如果你不能指出今天哪个机构是种族主义的。

00:57:11.440 --> 00:57:16.370
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Yeah, I mean, I have. But let's move on here because I have other questions, and I want you to be aware of your time.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>是的，我是说，我已经指出过了。但我们继续吧，因为我还有其他问题，我想让你注意你的时间。

00:57:16.370 --> 00:57:16.990
<v Alex Andrade>Whoa, Emmanuel, Emmanuel.
<v Alex Andrade>哇，伊曼纽尔，伊曼纽尔。

00:57:16.990 --> 00:57:19.060
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I have. Come on, mate.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我已经指出过了。来吧，伙计。

00:57:19.060 --> 00:57:19.840
<v Alex Andrade>No, you haven't.
<v Alex Andrade>不，你没有。

00:57:19.840 --> 00:57:45.190
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I don't know. I would disagree with that. And a big part of teaching also is just acknowledging where the weight of the scholarship is. And if the weight of the scholarship, which it does, says that a lot of our systems in this country are inherently unequal because initially they were not necessarily built to serve everybody, that just kind of is what it is.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我不知道。我不同意这一点。教学的一大部分也只是承认学术研究的分量在哪里。如果学术研究的分量——确实如此——说这个国家的很多系统本质上是不平等的，因为最初它们不一定是为了服务每个人而建立的，那也就那样了。

00:57:45.190 --> 00:58:03.080
<v Alex Andrade>There's no disagreement that Jim Crow laws were racist, that are evidence of systemic racism. However, if anyone is saying today, currently our institutions are inherently racist--
<v Alex Andrade>毫无疑问，吉姆·克劳法是种族主义的，那是系统性种族主义的证据。然而，如果今天有人说，我们目前的制度本质上是种族主义的——

00:58:03.080 --> 00:58:05.720
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Yeah, that's where you would disagree.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>是的，那是你会不同意的地方。

00:58:05.720 --> 00:58:08.770
<v Alex Andrade>Because it's just false.
<v Alex Andrade>因为它只是假的。

00:58:08.770 --> 00:58:16.160
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Right, well, we're disagreeing. I don't think it's false. I know it's not. But thank you for sharing that.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>好吧，我们正在分歧。我不认为它是假的。我知道它不是。但谢谢你的分享。

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:32.600
<v Ira Glass>He did this a few times. Presented with the weight of the evidence, he'd just say, there's no evidence, or he'd mention a study that he'd seen. And this is what's making life for professors so hard. They do already present clear and fact-based evidence.
<v Ira Glass>他这样做过几次。面对证据的分量，他只会说，没有证据，或者提到他见过的一项研究。这就是让教授们的生活变得如此艰难的原因。他们确实已经提出了清晰且基于事实的证据。

00:58:32.600 --> 00:58:57.210
<v Ira Glass>But Andrade is telling them they can't state what their evidence adds up to if it's a conclusion he and the Republican legislature don't agree with. How do you navigate that? As one professor told me, we can't just get up there and be like, so there's these discrepancies in health outcomes, but I can't tell you why. That's just not teaching.
<v Ira Glass>但安德拉德告诉他们，如果这是一个他和共和党立法机构不同意的结论，他们就不能陈述他们的证据加起来意味着什么。你怎么应对那个？正如一位教授告诉我的，我们不能只是站在那里像是，所以健康结果有这些差异，但我不能告诉你为什么。那根本不是教学。

00:58:57.210 --> 00:59:27.830
<v Ira Glass>I reached out to the DeSantis administration to ask them about HB 999. They didn't directly respond to my questions. Instead, their press secretary sent me links to a DeSantis press conference and a couple of other public statements about the bill. And the message from those was pretty clear. If you want to teach stuff about critical race theory or gender ideology, we're not going to be funding it. Go somewhere else.
<v Ira Glass>我联系了德桑蒂斯政府询问他们关于 HB 999 的事。他们没有直接回答我的问题。相反，他们的发言人给我发了德桑蒂斯新闻发布会的链接以及关于该法案的其他几个公开声明。那些声明传达的信息非常明确。如果你想教关于批判性种族理论或性别意识形态的东西，我们不会资助。去别的地方吧。

00:59:27.830 --> 00:59:37.340
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Gaines said that professors heard that message loud and clear. Students did too. Kaysin, for sure, she was following the news about the bill.
<v Ira Glass>盖恩斯博士说教授们清清楚楚地听到了那个信息。学生们也是。凯辛当然也是，她一直在关注关于该法案的新闻。

00:59:37.340 --> 00:59:43.520
<v Alisha Gaines>She came up and approached me at the end of my class. And she was just very upset. She said, what are you going to do about it?
<v Alisha Gaines>下课后她走过来接近我。她非常难过。她说，你要怎么做？

00:59:43.520 --> 00:59:46.280
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>What did she mean by that?
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>她是什么意思？

00:59:46.280 --> 01:00:02.340
<v Alisha Gaines>I think she meant, are you going to curtail what you have to say or what we're going to read or teach? Are you going to roll over?
<v Alisha Gaines>我想她是说，你是要缩减你要说的话，或者我们要阅读或教授的东西吗？你要屈服吗？

01:00:02.340 --> 01:00:05.160
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Kaysin said she actually meant something a little different.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>凯辛说她实际上指的意思有一点点不同。

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:12.420
<v Kaysin>I wanted to know how she was handling it because I kind of wanted to know how I should handle it, almost.
<v Kaysin>我想知道她是怎么处理它的，因为我有点想知道我应该怎么处理它。

01:00:12.420 --> 01:00:22.740
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Oh, you were like, I need to ask her how she's handling it so I can know how seriously I should be taking this, how afraid I should be?
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>噢，你是说，我需要问她是怎么处理的，以便我知道我应该多严肃地对待这件事，我应该多害怕？

01:00:22.740 --> 01:00:27.930
<v Kaysin>Yeah.
<v Kaysin>是的。

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:39.120
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Dr. Gaines told Kaysin she wasn't going to change her class at all, but she'd been feeling the pressure. A few weeks earlier, she decided to show a documentary in both of the classes she was teaching that semester.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>盖恩斯博士告诉凯辛她根本不会改变她的课程，但她一直感到压力。几周前，她决定在那学期她教的两门课中都放映一部纪录片。

01:00:39.120 --> 01:00:57.090
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>The documentary was called Invisible History, Middle Florida's Hidden Roots, and it was a documentary about the legacy of slavery around Leon County, where FSU is located. The first class she showed it to was all grad students. And when the film was finished, there was this stunned silence in the room.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>这部纪录片叫《隐形历史：佛罗里达中部的隐藏根源》（Invisible History, Middle Florida's Hidden Roots），是关于利昂县（Leon County，佛罗里达州立大学所在地）奴隶制遗留问题的纪录片。她放给研究生上的第一堂课，当电影放完时，房间里是一片震惊的寂静。

01:00:57.090 --> 01:01:00.840
<v Alisha Gaines>One of my graduate students said, well, DeSantis didn't want us to see that.
<v Alisha Gaines>我的一位研究生说，好吧，德桑蒂斯不想让我们看那个。

01:01:00.840 --> 01:01:19.500
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>As in, Dr. Gaines, you just did something that could get you in trouble. Dr. Gaines was surprised. This was just a fact-based documentary that had been made by a filmmaker in residence at FSU, though it is pretty unflinching in the way it ties Leon County's Jim Crow past with its present.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>意思是，盖恩斯博士，你刚才做了一件可能会让你惹上麻烦的事。盖恩斯博士很惊讶。这只是一部基于事实的纪录片，由佛罗里达州立大学的一位驻校电影制作人制作，尽管它在将利昂县的吉姆·克劳过去与现在联系起来的方式上相当直白。

01:01:19.500 --> 01:01:26.850
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Half an hour later, she screened the same documentary for Kaysin's class. And they were in the middle of a film when she noticed something.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>半小时后，她为凯辛的班级放映了同一部纪录片。当他们正看到电影中间时，她注意到了什么。

01:01:26.850 --> 01:01:54.890
<v Alisha Gaines>I think the documentary was about-- had gotten to a point about lynching. So it was a very heavy moment. I'm not going to say it wasn't. That history is hard. That's painful. And that history is right here in Leon County itself. And there was a moment where a student walked out of the classroom while it was screening. I literally kind of jumped in my seat because I wasn't sure why he was leaving.
<v Alisha Gaines>我想纪录片是关于——已经到了关于私刑的一点。所以那是一个非常沉重的时刻。我不会说它不是。那段历史很沉重。那是痛苦的。而且那段历史就在利昂县本身。有一个瞬间，一名学生在放映时走出了教室。我简直在座位上跳了一下，因为我不确定他为什么离开。

01:01:54.890 --> 01:01:56.690
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Dr. Gaines started panicking.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>盖恩斯博士开始恐慌。

01:01:56.690 --> 01:02:16.930
<v Alisha Gaines>I was immediately thinking, is he walking out because he's protesting what I'm screening? Is he walking out because he is made so very uncomfortable by this documentary about slavery in Leon County or Middle Florida? All of these things are kind of racing through my head.
<v Alisha Gaines>我立刻在想，他走出去是因为抗议我放映的东西吗？他走出去是因为这部关于利昂县或佛罗里达中部奴隶制的纪录片让他感到如此不舒服吗？所有这些事情在我的脑海中奔腾。

01:02:16.930 --> 01:02:33.280
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Gaines wasn't sure what would happen if she had offended his kid. So she wrote down some notes about where exactly in the film he'd walked out and then sat there, anxiously looking at the other students to see if anyone else was going to follow. And then--
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>盖恩斯不确定如果她冒犯了他的孩子会发生什么。所以她写下了一些关于他在电影中确切哪里走出的笔记，然后坐在那里，焦虑地看着其他学生，看看是否还有人会跟随。然后——

01:02:33.280 --> 01:02:47.710
<v Alisha Gaines>That student came back a few minutes later. He had just gone to the restroom. And so I'm having this, [GASPS] oh, gosh. He was just using the restroom.
<v Alisha Gaines>那名学生几分钟后回来了。他只是去了洗手间。所以我经历了那个，[喘气] 噢，天哪。他只是用了个洗手间。

01:02:47.710 --> 01:02:58.540
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>I checked with the student, and he hadn't actually gone to the bathroom. He stepped into the hall because of some news he'd gotten. But Dr. Gaines was seeing threats where there weren't any. She was beginning to doubt herself.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>我向学生核实了，他其实并没有去洗手间。他走到大厅是因为他接到的一些消息。但盖恩斯博士看到了不存在的威胁。她开始怀疑自己。

01:02:58.540 --> 01:03:12.300
<v Alisha Gaines>It's that double think that I have never had to do in a classroom in my entire career.
<v Alisha Gaines>那种双重思维（double think）是我整个职业生涯中在课堂上从未不得不做过的。

01:03:12.300 --> 01:03:23.490
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>By early April, as the bill was being debated in committee, Kaysin was finding it increasingly difficult to stay upbeat as she saw news about the bill's progress. It had been a rough semester.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>到 4 月初，当法案在委员会中被辩论时，凯辛发现，随着她看到关于法案进展的新闻，她越来越难以保持乐观。那是一个艰难的学期。

01:03:23.490 --> 01:03:35.510
<v Kaysin>I was trying my best to enjoy it, even though there was a kind of constant backdrop of stress with everything happening. And I thought about dropping out of college.
<v Kaysin>我尽力享受它，尽管所有发生的事情都有持续的压力背景。我想过退学。

01:03:35.510 --> 01:03:36.720
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Wait, wait, wait.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>等等，等等，等等。

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:48.270
<v Kaysin>Yeah, it ran through my head several times. Even though I enjoyed it, even though I was so focused on getting to class, in my head, several times, I was like, maybe I should just bail out.
<v Kaysin>是的，这在我脑海中闪过几次。尽管我很享受，尽管我很专注于上课，但在我脑海中，几次我都想，也许我应该直接放弃。

01:03:48.270 --> 01:04:33.440
<v Kaysin>It's kind of like if someone gives you a beautiful present, like they give you a beautiful watch. And they say, hey, I might take this away at any second. And when I take it away, I'm just straight up going to mug you. Would you accept the watch? Would you want to wear it on your wrist, so that after it's gone, you'd have to see the place where it was, that little tan line where the watch was? Would you want to still accept that watch when it would feel safer to not have it at all?
<v Kaysin>这有点像是如果有人给你一份精美的礼物，比如他们给了你一块精美的手表。然后他们说，嘿，我可能随时把它拿走。当把它拿走时，我就直接去抢你。你会接受那块手表吗？你会想把它戴在手腕上，这样当它没了之后，你还得看着它曾待过的地方，那个手表曾在那里的晒痕？你还想接受那块手表吗，即使不拥有它会感到更安全？

01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:54.230
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Right after the semester finished, DeSantis signed his big higher ed package. That final law did not have some of the things the professors I spoke with found most alarming. They dropped the idea that no one could major or minor in gender studies or feminist theory or critical race theory. They dropped the idea that unruly professors could have their tenure reviewed at any time.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>学期刚结束，德桑蒂斯签署了他的大型高等教育一揽子计划。那部最终法律并没有包含我交谈过的教授们认为最令人担忧的一些东西。他们放弃了没有人能主修或辅修性别研究、女性主义理论或批判性种族理论的想法。他们放弃了不守规矩的教授可以随时被审查终身职位的想法。

01:04:54.230 --> 01:05:19.920
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>But the law does say that in general education courses, you can't teach identity politics or that systemic racism or sexism are built into the institutions of the United States. Kaysin saw the news about the bill passing and thought about her future. They're not going to stop here, she told me. Who knows what further restrictions to higher education they might make next year? Would you want to stick around for that?
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>但法律确实说，在通识教育课程中，你不能教授身份政治，或者不能教授系统性种族主义或性别歧视是美国制度的内在组成部分。凯辛看到了关于法案通过的新闻，想到了她的未来。他们不会在这里停止，她告诉我。谁知道明年他们还会对高等教育做出什么进一步的限制？你会想留下来面对吗？

01:05:19.920 --> 01:05:29.880
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>It's a thing she's talking about a lot nowadays with the people in her life, with her friends, with her parents, just what staying or leaving would mean, especially as a Black person in the South.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>这是她现在和她生活中的人们、朋友、父母谈论很多的事情，关于留下或离开意味着什么，特别是在南方作为一个黑人。

01:05:29.880 --> 01:05:51.260
<v Kaysin>I was in that particular headspace when I went to my dad. And I was like, I think I need to get out of this state. I don't think I should stay here whatsoever. I don't feel safe. And he took that in. Then he was to me, like, but we don't run from things like that.
<v Kaysin>当我去找我父亲时，我处于那种特定的心态中。我说，我想我需要离开这个州。我不认为我应该留在这里。我不觉得安全。他听进去了。然后他对我像是，但我们不躲避那样的事情。

01:05:51.260 --> 01:06:22.300
<v Kaysin>And I was like, dude, what are you talking about? And he was like, we're Black people. We're living in the South. If we left every time we felt threatened, no one would be here right now. And it was just this idea of, when people are trying to scare you away, you don't just go along with it. You don't do them the favor of leaving.
<v Kaysin>我说，伙计，你在说什么？他说，我们是黑人。我们生活在南方。如果我们每次感到受到威胁都离开，现在这里一个人都不会留下了。这只是一种想法，当人们试图把你吓走时，你不要只是顺从它。你不要帮他们离开。

01:06:22.300 --> 01:06:35.590
<v Kaysin>You stay, and you try to make things right. And if you do leave, it's because you found something that you definitely know is better. You leave for you, not for them.
<v Kaysin>你留下来，努力纠正事情。如果你确实离开，那是因为你发现了一些你绝对确定更好的东西。你为你自己而离开，而不是为了他们。

01:06:35.590 --> 01:06:47.290
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Whether or not Kaysin stays in Florida through grad school, the one thing she knows for sure is that she still wants to be a professor, just like Dr. Gaines. The new law didn't change that.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>无论凯辛是否在佛罗里达州读完研究生，她唯一确定的是她仍然想成为一名教授，就像盖恩斯博士一样。新法律并没有改变这一点。

01:06:47.290 --> 01:07:06.040
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>It did have a chilling effect. But what that looks like right now among the professors I talk to is people feeling fear, maybe changing their course names or removing things from their CVs. But mostly, these educators are standing their ground and teaching the same things they were before.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>它确实产生了寒蝉效应。但在我交谈过的教授中，这目前看起来像是人们感到恐惧，可能会改变课程名称或从他们的简历中删除一些东西。但大多数情况下，这些教育工作者们坚持阵地，教授着他们以前教的内容。

01:07:06.040 --> 01:07:34.950
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>Dr. Gaines, for instance, that class about major American writers, she decided to go ahead and teach only Black authors from the Harlem Renaissance. Dr. Celia Caputi is moving ahead with her article referencing the lesbian continuum. The faculty union is telling everyone, don't change your classes. If they try to use the law to stop you, we'll fight.
<v Emmanuel Dzotsi>例如，盖恩斯博士那门关于美国主要作家的课，她决定继续只教哈莱姆文艺复兴时期的黑人作家。西莉亚·卡普蒂博士正在继续进行她关于女同性恋连续体的文章。教师工会告诉每个人，不要改变你的课程。如果他们试图利用法律来阻止你，我们会斗争。

01:07:34.950 --> 01:07:42.180
<v Ira Glass>Reporter Emmanuel Dzotsi.
<v Ira Glass>记者伊曼纽尔·佐齐。

01:07:42.180 --> 01:07:44.370
<v Ira Glass>Act three, Goodbye Sunshine.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，再见阳光。

01:07:44.370 --> 01:07:54.760
<v Eliza>So here's my bedroom. My suitcase is out on my bed, and then I just have three boxes left to unpack-- actually, no, two boxes left to unpack.
<v Eliza>好了，这是我的卧室。我的手提箱在床上，然后我只有三个箱子没拆——事实上，不，还有两个箱子没拆。

01:07:54.760 --> 01:08:08.340
<v Ira Glass>So we started our show with people moving to Florida. We end with somebody moving away. Eliza's 16, just finished her sophomore year of high school. And I talked to her two days after she arrived in the house her family just moved to in Virginia.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们以人们搬到佛罗里达州开始了我们的节目。我们以某人搬走而结束。伊丽莎（Eliza）16 岁，刚上完高中二年级。我在她搬进她在弗吉尼亚州的新房子的两天后和她交谈。

01:08:08.340 --> 01:08:18.720
<v Ira Glass>They moved because of the law that Ron DeSantis enacted in May that bans gender-affirming care for anybody under 18. Eliza's trans, takes a hormone blocker, an estrogen.
<v Ira Glass>他们搬家是因为罗恩·德桑蒂斯在五月制定的法律，禁止 18 岁以下的人进行性别肯定护理。伊丽莎是跨性别者，服用激素阻断剂，雌激素。

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:33.720
<v Eliza>Yeah, it was just, the a concern is that I wouldn't be able to get the care that I needed. So we had to move somewhere where we knew that that wouldn't be threatened, at least for another couple of years.
<v Eliza>是的，只是，担心我无法获得我需要的护理。所以我们必须搬到某个地方，我们知道那至少在未来几年内不会受到威胁。

01:08:33.720 --> 01:08:48.340
<v Ira Glass>Eliza's dad is in the military, so she's moved around a lot, Ohio, Texas, Germany, Los Angeles, Huntsville, Montgomery, been to lots of schools. She says, usually there's an even split between people who believe different kinds of things, but not in her school in Florida.
<v Ira Glass>伊丽莎的父亲在军队服役，所以她经常搬家，俄亥俄州、得克萨斯州、德国、洛杉矶、亨茨维尔、蒙哥马利，去过很多学校。她说，通常在相信不同事情的人之间会有均等的划分，但在她在佛罗里达州的学校里却没有。

01:08:48.340 --> 01:09:16.990
<v Eliza>It was just very-- I feel like the entire scale was shifted to the right. I feel like even the most progressive people at my school were still way, way less progressive than any other place that I've been in before. It's just so casual, just really common, really normal to just be awful and then to be exclusionary to people.
<v Eliza>它只是非常——我觉得整个天平向右倾斜了。我觉得即使是我学校里最进步的人，也比我以前去过的任何其他地方都进步得多，得多。这太随意了，非常普遍，非常正常，只是变得糟糕，然后排斥别人。

01:09:16.990 --> 01:09:23.800
<v Eliza>But I just got kind of used to it. I don't know. Florida's just going to be Florida.
<v Eliza>但我只是习惯了。我不知道。佛罗里达州注定就是佛罗里达州。

01:09:23.800 --> 01:09:42.020
<v Ira Glass>We're putting this interview with you-- our conversation that we're having right now-- into an episode of our show that begins with people who moved to Florida because they liked the politics there. And I wonder, when you hear that, what do you think of people like that?
<v Ira Glass>我们正在把你这次采访——我们现在正在进行的对话——放入我们节目的一集中，这一集以那些因为喜欢那里的政治而搬到佛罗里达州的人开始。我想知道，当你听到这些时，你会怎么看待那些人？

01:09:42.020 --> 01:10:13.620
<v Eliza>Um, I think that is a crazy thing to hear. And it just shows-- I don't know. I feel like-- congratulations, that the things that are being threatened every day don't affect you. You're lucky. But that's not the case for me, and that's not the case for a lot of people.
<v Eliza>嗯，我觉得那是很疯狂的事情。这只是表明——我不知道。我觉得——恭喜，那些每天受到威胁的事情不会影响你。你很幸运。但对我来说不是这样，对很多人来说也不是这样。

01:10:13.620 --> 01:11:12.970
<v Ira Glass>Parts of the law that made Eliza move have been overturned by the courts. But that doesn't matter to her family. Eliza wasn't out to many people in Florida. Most kids at school didn't know she was trans. So when she had to explain to them the reason why she was leaving, she kept it simple. "Politics," she said.
<v Ira Glass>那项让伊丽莎搬家的法律的部分内容已被法院推翻。但这对她的家庭来说并不重要。伊丽莎在佛罗里达州没有向许多人出柜。学校里大多数孩子都不知道她是跨性别者。所以当她不得不向他们解释她离开的原因时，她说得很简单。“政治，”她说。

01:11:12.970 --> 01:11:15.450
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Zoe Chace and Alaa Mostafa.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我们的节目今天由佐伊·蔡斯和阿拉·穆斯塔法（Alaa Mostafa）制作。

01:11:15.450 --> 01:11:32.590
<v Ira Glass>The people put together today's show include Bim Adewunmi, Chris Benderev, James Bennett II, Phia Bennin, Sean Cole, Michael Comite, Aviva DeKornfeld, Bethel Habte, Cassie Howley, Valerie Kipnis, Seth Lind, Caitlin Love, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Rae Mondo, Nadia Reiman, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Lilly Sullivan, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, and Diane Wu.
<v Ira Glass>今天把节目凑在一起的人包括 Bim Adewunmi、Chris Benderev、James Bennett II、Phia Bennin、Sean Cole、Michael Comite、Aviva DeKornfeld、Bethel Habte、Cassie Howley、Valerie Kipnis、Seth Lind、Caitlin Love、Stowe Nelson、Katherine Rae Mondo、Nadia Reiman、Ryan Rumery、Alissa Shipp、Lilly Sullivan、Christopher Swetala、Matt Tierney 和 Diane Wu。

01:11:32.590 --> 01:11:40.690
<v Ira Glass>Managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our senior editor is David Kestenbaum. Our executive editor is Emmanuele Berry. Ike Sriskandarajah produced act three of our show.
<v Ira Glass>执行主编是 Sarah Abdurrahman。我们的高级编辑是 David Kestenbaum。我们的执行编辑是 Emmanuele Berry。Ike Sriskandarajah 制作了我们节目的第三幕。

01:11:40.690 --> 01:12:11.570
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Paul Westcott of L2 Data, Devan Vilfrard, Jacob Perry, Dominique Jean Louis, Taylor Cox, John Gregory, Laurel Bristow, the National Conference of State Legislatures, Julio Ochoa, Stephanie Colombini, Earle Kimel, Paul Offit, Kim Savage, Rob Brooks, Ann Davies, Christopher Spata from The Tampa Bay Times, Zac Anderson from the Sarasota Herald-Tribune, Tho Bishop, Paul and Brenda Chabot, Selene San Felice, Adam Steinbaugh, Aubrey Jewett, Susan MacManus, and Julia Manchester and Max Greenwood, who wrote about Florida turning red for The Hill.
<v Ira Glass>今天特别感谢 L2 Data 的 Paul Westcott、Devan Vilfrard、Jacob Perry、Dominique Jean Louis、Taylor Cox、John Gregory、Laurel Bristow、全国州议会会议、Julio Ochoa、Stephanie Colombini、Earle Kimel、Paul Offit、Kim Savage、Rob Brooks、Ann Davies、《坦帕湾时报》的 Christopher Spata、《萨拉索塔先驱论坛报》的 Zac Anderson、Tho Bishop、Paul 和 Brenda Chabot、Selene San Felice、Adam Steinbaugh、Aubrey Jewett、Susan MacManus，以及为《国会山报》撰写关于佛罗里达州变红的 Julia Manchester 和 Max Greenwood。

01:12:11.570 --> 01:12:29.630
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Our website, thisamericanlife.org, where we have new merch, including great new T-shirts and more. My favorite is this really beautiful illustration of our show's co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia. Check it out, thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》通过公共广播交流中心 PRX 提供给公共广播电台。我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org，我们在那里有新的商品，包括很棒的新 T 恤等等。我最喜欢的是我们节目联合创始人 Torey Malatia 先生这张非常漂亮的插图。去看看吧，thisamericanlife.org。

01:12:29.630 --> 01:12:37.130
<v Ira Glass>Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. He refuses to go to dinner and a movie with me if it's just the two of us. And here's why.
<v Ira Glass>像往常一样，感谢我们节目的联合创始人 Torey Malatia 先生。如果只有我们俩，他拒绝和我一起出去吃饭看电影。这就是原因。

01:12:37.130 --> 01:12:39.380
<v Tanya Parus>I don't believe in man dates.
<v Tanya Parus>我不相信强制令（man-dates）。

01:12:39.380 --> 01:12:44.380
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。
